<chapter xml:id="chapter-1">
  <title>Basic geometry in <m>\mathbb{N}_0 \times \mathbb{N}_0</m></title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      In this chapter we first give a detailed description of the geometry of the support of polynomials
      in <m>K[x,y]</m> and the relation with the plane Jacobian conjecture. 
      Then we introduce our main tools: directions and leading terms <m>\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m> 
      corresponding to these directions (see <xref ref="defino1_1"/>), 
      the transformation of the shape of the support by certain automorphisms of <m>K[x,y]</m>, and the very important element <m>F</m>. 
      The main result of this chapter is that if the Jacobian conjecture is false, then <m>B \ge 9</m>, 
      where <m>B := \min\bigl(\gcd(\deg(P),\deg(Q))\bigr)</m>, and <m>(P,Q)</m> runs on the counterexamples. 
      This result was already proven by Nagata in <cite>Nagata</cite>. In order to prove <m>B \ge 9</m>, we consider
      a counterexample <m>(P,Q)</m> such that <m>B = \gcd(\deg(P),\deg(Q))</m>. We transform this counterexample into 
      a standard <m>(m,n)</m>-pair in <m>K[x,y]</m> (see <xref ref="defino1_2"/>). For the transformed pair there exist 
      <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous elements (see <xref ref="defino1_3"/>) <m>R</m> and <m>F</m> 
      satisfying very strong conditions (see <xref ref="defino1_4"/>). The supports of <m>R</m> and <m>F</m> 
      satisfying these conditions, with minimum <m>\deg(R)</m>, are shown in <xref ref="img1-1"/>.
    </p> 
      <figure xml:id="img1-1">
        <image width="30%" source="img/img1.png"/>
        <caption>Minimal possible support of F and R</caption>
      </figure>

    <p><m>\deg(R) = 9</m>, which implies <m>B \geq 9</m>. The key point of the proof is the transformation of the minimal 
      counterexample into a standard <m>(m,n)</m>-pair, which needs the most amount of work. This transformation is carried 
      out using automorphisms of the algebra <m>K[x,y]</m>, which alter the support of the polynomials—subsets 
      of <m>\mathbb{N}_0 \times \mathbb{N}_0</m>—through basic geometric operations on the plane.
      The shaping of the support depends on the multiplicity of the linear factors in the polynomials <m>\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m>, 
      which are strongly conditioned by the multiplicity of these linear factors in the <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous polynomial 
      <m>F</m> (see <xref ref="defino1_5"/>).
      This underlines the importance of the element <m>F</m>, which is the crucial new tool of our whole approach.
    </p>
 </introduction>

  <!-- ===================== -->
  <!-- SECTION 1.1 -->     \operatorname{Supp}(P) := \{(i,j) : a_{ij} \neq 0\}
  <!-- ===================== -->

  <section xml:id="sec-1-1">
    <title>Polynomials in two variables and their support</title>
    <p>Polynomials in two variables over a field <m>K</m> are expressions of the form</p>
    <me>
      P(x,y) = \sum_{i,j \in \mathbb{N}_0} a_{ij} x^i y^j
    </me>
    <p>For example:</p>
    <me>
      P = 3x^3 + 2x^2 + 4xy + 7y^2 + 2y - 8
    </me>
    <p> The <term>support</term> of a polynomial is defined as: </p>

    <me>
      \operatorname{Supp} (P) := \{(i,j) : a_{ij} \neq 0\}
    </me>

    <figure xml:id="img1-2">
      <image width="100%" source="img/img2.png"/>   //width="400px"   height="300px"
      <caption>Support of P</caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The algebra <m>K[x,y]</m> has basis 
      <m>\{x^i y^j : (i,j) \in \mathbb{N}_0^2\}</m>.
    </p>

    <me>
      x^i y^j \cdot x^k y^l = x^{i+k} y^{j+l}
    </me>
    <p>
      on elements of the basis, and then extended by bilinearity to arbitrary polynomials. If one of <m>P</m> or <m>Q</m> is a monomial, then the support of the product is the sum of the supports. Here we define the <term>sum</term> and the <term>difference</term> of two subsets <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> of <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>, by
    </p>

    <me>
      A + B := \{a + b : a \in A \text{ and } b \in B\}
      \quad \text{and} \quad
      A - B := \{a - b : a \in A \text{ and } b \in B\},
    </me>

    <p>
      respectively. For example, if
    </p>

    <me>
      P = 3x^3 + 2x^2 + 4xy + 7y^2 + 2y - 8
      \quad \text{and} \quad
      Q = x^3 y,
    </me>
    <p> then  </p>
    <me>PQ=3x^6y+ 2x^5y +4x^4y^2+7x^3y^3 +2x^3y^2-8x^3y</me>,
    <p>
    and so <m>\operatorname{Supp}(PQ) = \operatorname{Supp}(P)+\operatorname{Supp}(Q)</m>. The supports of <m>P</m>, 
    <m>Q</m> and <m>PQ</m> are shown in <xref ref="fig_3"/>.
    </p>
  <figure xml:id="fig_3">
        <image width="100%" source="img/img3.png"/>
       <caption>Example of Supports of <m>P</m>, <m>Q</m> and <m>PQ</m>, with <m>Q</m> a monomial</caption>
      </figure>

      
 

  <p>
    In general, we only have
    <men xml:id="soporte_de_PQ_incluido_en_soporte_de_P_mas_soporte_de_Q">
      \operatorname{Supp}(PQ)\subseteq \operatorname{Supp}(P)+\operatorname{Supp}(Q)
    </men>.
    For example, take
    <men xml:id="ejemplo1">
      P=3xy-y^2+3y+3\quad\text{and}\quad Q=x^4y^2-3x^4y-2x^3y^2+2x^3y
    </men>.
    Then
    <md>
      <mrow>
        \operatorname{Supp}(P)=\{(1,1),(0,2),(0,1),(0,0)\},\qquad 
        \operatorname{Supp}(Q)=\{(4,2),(4,1), (3,2),(3,1)\},
      </mrow>
      <intertext>and so</intertext>
      <mrow>
        \operatorname{Supp}(P)+\operatorname{Supp}(Q)=\{(5,3),(4,4),(5,2),(4,3),(3,4),(4,2),(3,3),(4,1),(3,2),(3,1)\}.
      </mrow>
    </md>
    But
    <me>
      PQ=3x^5y^3-x^4y^4-9x^5y^2+2x^3y^4-8x^3y^3-9x^4y+6x^3y
    </me>,
    and so
    <me>
      \operatorname{Supp}(PQ)=\{(5,3),(4,4),(5,2),(3,4),(3,3),(4,1),(3,1)\}
    </me>,
    which shows that <m>\operatorname{Supp}(PQ)\ne \operatorname{Supp}(P)+\operatorname{Supp}(Q)</m> (see <xref ref="newton_contenido"/>).
  </p>
<figure xml:id="newton_contenido">
        <image width="100%" source="img/img4.png"/>
       <caption>A case in which <m>\operatorname{Supp}(PQ)\subsetneq \operatorname{Supp}(P)+\operatorname{Supp}(Q)</m></caption>
   
      </figure>


    <definition xml:id="poligono_de_Newton">
      <title>Newton Polygon</title>
      <p>
        The <term>Newton polygon</term> <idx>Newton polygon</idx> <m>\operatorname{H}(P)</m>, of a polynomial <m>P</m>, 
        is the convex hull of the support of <m>P</m>.
      </p>
    </definition>
    <p>
      Since <m>\operatorname{HN}(P) + \operatorname{HN}(Q)</m> is a convex set, from 
      <xref ref="soporte_de_PQ_incluido_en_soporte_de_P_mas_soporte_de_Q"/> it follows that 
      <m>\operatorname{HN}(PQ) \subseteq \operatorname{HN}(P) + \operatorname{HN}(Q)</m>. 
      We will see in <xref ref="soporte_de_PQ_incluido_en_soporte_de_P_mas_soporte_de_Q"/> that the equality holds, as it is illustrated in 
      <xref ref="fig_5"/>, where <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> are as in <xref ref="ejemplo1"/>.
    </p>

<figure xml:id="fig_5">
          <image width="80%" source="img/img5.png"/>
          <caption>Newton polygon of the product of two polynomials</caption>
        </figure>


  </section>




  <!-- ===================== -->
  <!-- SECTION 1.2 -->
  <!-- ===================== -->

  <section xml:id="sec-1-2">
    <title>The Jacobian determinant and its support</title>
    <definition xml:id="derivada_parcial_respecto_de_x">
   
    <p>
      The (formal) partial derivative of <m>P=\sum_{i,j} a_{ij}x^i y^j</m> with respect to <m>x</m> 
      <idx>partial derivative with respect to <m>x</m></idx> is
      <me>
        \partial_x(P)=P_x=\frac{\partial P}{\partial x} := \sum_{i,j} i a_{ij}x^{i-1} y^j.
      </me>
    </p>
  </definition>

  <p>
    For example <m>\partial_x(x^3y)=3x^2y</m>, and so 
    <m>\operatorname{Supp}(\partial_x(x^3y)) = \{(2,1)\} = \operatorname{Supp}(x^3y)-(1,0)</m> 
    (see <xref ref="fig_6"/>).
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig_6">
    <caption><m>\operatorname{Supp}(x^3y)</m> and <m>\operatorname{Supp}(\partial_x(x^3y))</m></caption>
    <image width="40%" source="img/img6.png"/>
  </figure>

  <p>
    We suspect that <m>\operatorname{Supp}(P_x) = \operatorname{Supp}(P)-(1,0)</m>. But, we have 
    <m>\partial_x(y)=0</m>, and so,
    <me>
      \operatorname{Supp}(\partial_x(y)) = \emptyset \ne \operatorname{Supp}(y)-(1,0).
    </me>
    The correct formula is
    <me>
      \operatorname{Supp}(P_x) = \left(\operatorname{Supp}(P)-(1,0)\right) \cap \mathbb{N}_0^2.
    </me>
  </p>

  <definition xml:id="derivada_parcial_respecto_de_y">
   
    <p>
      The partial derivative of <m>P=\sum_{i,j} a_{ij}x^i y^j</m> with respect to <m>y</m>
      <idx>partial derivative with respect to <m>y</m></idx> is
      <me>
        \partial_y(P)=P_y=\frac{\partial P}{\partial y} := \sum_{i,j} j a_{ij} x^i y^{j-1}.
      </me>
    </p>
  </definition>

  <p>
    A similar discussion as above shows that the support of <m>P_y</m> is given by
    <me>
      \operatorname{Supp}(P_y) = \left(\operatorname{Supp}(P)-(0,1)\right) \cap \mathbb{N}_0^2.
    </me>
    For example, if <m>P = x^5(y^3+y^2) + x^4(y^4 + y^3 + y^2 + y) + x^3(y^4 + y^3 + y^2 + y + 1)</m>, then
    <md>
      <mrow>
        P_x = 5x^4(y^3+y^2) + 4x^3(y^4 + y^3 + y^2 + y) + 3x^2(y^4 + y^3 + y^2 + y + 1)
      </mrow>
      <intertext>and</intertext>
      <mrow>
        P_y = x^5(3y^2+2y) + x^4(4y^3 + 3y^2 + 2y + 1) + x^3(4y^3 + 3y^2 + 2y + 1).
      </mrow>
    </md>
    We show the supports of <m>P</m>, <m>P_x</m> and <m>P_y</m> in <xref ref="fig_7"/>.
  </p>

<figure xml:id="fig_7">
        <image width="85%" source="img/img7.png"/>
        <caption>Supports of a polynomial and its partial derivatives</caption>
      </figure>


 

  <definition xml:id="aplicaciones_polinomiales">
    <title>Polynomial Maps</title>
    <p>
      A map <m>f\colon K^2 \to K^2</m> is called a <term>polynomial map</term> 
      <idx>polynomial map</idx> if there exist <m>P,Q \in K[x,y]</m> such that 
      <m>f(a,b) = (P(a,b), Q(a,b))</m>, for all <m>(a,b) \in K^2</m>. 
      From now on we will name <m>f_{P,Q}</m> the polynomial map <m>f</m>.
    </p>
  </definition>

  <definition xml:id="matriz_jacobiana">
    <title>Jacobian Matrix</title>
    <p>
      The <term>Jacobian matrix</term> <idx>Jacobian matrix</idx> of <m>f = f_{P,Q}</m> is
      <me>
        \operatorname{D} f := \begin{pmatrix} P_x &amp; P_y \\ Q_x &amp; Q_y \end{pmatrix}.
      </me>
      We write
      <me>
        [P,Q] = \operatorname{J}(P,Q) := \det \operatorname{D} f = P_x Q_y - P_y Q_x,
      </me>
      and call it the <term>Jacobian determinant</term> or simply the <term>Jacobian</term> 
      <idx>Jacobian</idx> of <m>(P,Q)</m>. Moreover, for <m>P,Q,R,S \in K[x,y]</m>, we set
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \operatorname{D} f_{\,\vert (R,S)} := \begin{pmatrix} P_x(R,S) &amp; P_y(R,S) \\ Q_x(R,S) &amp; Q_y(R,S) \end{pmatrix}
        </mrow>
        <intertext>and</intertext>
        <mrow>
          \operatorname{J}(P,Q)_{\vert (R,S)} := \det(\operatorname{D} f)_{\vert (R,S)} = P_x(R,S) Q_y(R,S) - P_y(R,S) Q_x(R,S).
        </mrow>
      </md>
      Note that <m>\operatorname{D} f_{\,\vert (x,y)} = \operatorname{D} f</m> and 
      <m>\operatorname{J}(P,Q)_{\vert (x,y)} = \operatorname{J}(P,Q) = [P,Q]</m>.
    </p>
  </definition>

  <p>
    Since <m>\operatorname{Supp}(P_x) \subseteq \operatorname{Supp}(P)-(1,0)</m> and 
    <m>\operatorname{Supp}(Q_y) \subseteq \operatorname{Supp}(Q)-(0,1)</m>, we have
    <me>
      \operatorname{Supp}(P_x Q_y) \subseteq \operatorname{Supp}(P) + \operatorname{Supp}(Q) - (1,1),
    </me>
    and similarly
    <me>
      \operatorname{Supp}(P_y Q_x) \subseteq \operatorname{Supp}(P) + \operatorname{Supp}(Q) - (1,1).
    </me>
  </p>
    <p>
    So,
    <men xml:id="inclusion_de_supp_P_Q">
      \operatorname{Supp}([P,Q]) \subseteq \operatorname{Supp}(P) + \operatorname{Supp}(Q) - (1,1).
    </men>
    Note that one could also use that
    <men xml:id="determinante">
      [x^i y^j, x^k y^l] = (il - jk) x^{i+k-1} y^{j+l-1},
    </men>
    in order to obtain the same conclusion. For example, if
    <men xml:id="P_y_Q_particulares">
    P = 3xy - y^2 + 3y + 3 \quad\text{and}\quad Q = x^4 y^2 - 3x^4 y - 2x^3 y^2 + 2x^3 y,
    </men>
    then
    <me>
      [P,Q] = -6x^4 y^2 + 8x^3 y^3 + 27x^4 y - 30x^3 y^2 - 12x^2 y^3 + 24x^3 y + 30x^2 y^2 - 18x^2 y.
    </me>
    So,
    <md>
      <mrow>
        \operatorname{Supp}(P) = \{(1,1),(0,2),(0,1),(0,0)\},\quad 
        \operatorname{Supp}(Q) = \{(4,2),(4,1),(3,2),(3,1)\}
      </mrow>
      <intertext>and</intertext>
      <mrow>
        \operatorname{Supp}([P,Q]) = \{(4,2),(3,3),(4,1),(3,2),(2,3),(3,1),(2,2),(2,1)\}.
      </mrow>
    </md>
    As it is shown in <xref ref="fig_8"/>, in this example the inclusion 
    <xref ref="inclusion_de_supp_P_Q"/> is strict.
  </p>

<figure xml:id="fig_8">
        <image width="80%" source="img/img8.png"/>
         <caption>A case in which the inclusion <xref ref="inclusion_de_supp_P_Q"/> is strict</caption>
      </figure>




  <proposition xml:id="H_P_Q_subset_H_P_plus_H_Q_minus_1">
    <p>
      For each <m>P,Q \in K[x,y]</m>, we have 
      <m>\operatorname{HN}([P,Q]) \subseteq \operatorname{HN}(P) + \operatorname{HN}(Q) - (1,1)</m>.
    </p>
</proposition>

  <proof>
    <p>
      By identity <xref ref="inclusion_de_supp_P_Q"/> and the fact that 
      <m>\operatorname{Supp}(P) \subseteq \operatorname{HN}(P)</m> and 
      <m>\operatorname{Supp}(Q) \subseteq \operatorname{HN}(Q)</m>, we know that
      <me>
        \operatorname{Supp}([P,Q]) \subseteq \operatorname{Supp}(P) + \operatorname{Supp}(Q) - (1,1)
        \subseteq \operatorname{HN}(P) + \operatorname{HN}(Q) - (1,1).
      </me>
      The result follows immediately from this fact, since 
      <m>\operatorname{HN}(P) + \operatorname{HN}(Q) - (1,1)</m> is a convex set.
    </p>
  </proof>

  <p>
    In <xref ref="fig_9"/>, we show that for <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> as in 
    <xref ref="P_y_Q_particulares"/>, the inclusion in 
    <xref ref="H_P_Q_subset_H_P_plus_H_Q_minus_1"/> is strict.
  </p>


<figure xml:id="fig_9">
        <image width="80%" source="img/img9.png"/>
        <caption>A case in which the inclusion in 
      <xref ref="H_P_Q_subset_H_P_plus_H_Q_minus_1"/> is strict</caption>
      </figure>



  <lemma xml:id="propiedades_basicas_del_jacobiano">
  <title>Basic Properties of the Jacobian</title>
  <statement>
    <p>For each <m>P,Q,H \in K[x,y]</m> the following facts hold:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><m>[-,-]</m> is a <m>K</m>-bilinear and alternate form.</li>
      <li><m>[PQ,H] = [P,H]Q + P[Q,H]</m>.</li>
      <li><m>[P^m, Q] = m P^{m-1} [P,Q]</m>, for all <m>m \in \mathbb{N}</m>.</li>
      <li><m>[H, PQ] = [H,P]Q + P[H,Q]</m>.</li>
      <li><m>[P, Q^n] = n Q^{n-1} [P,Q]</m>, for all <m>n \in \mathbb{N}</m>.</li>
    </ol>
  </statement>
</lemma>
    <proof>
    <p>Item 1 is straightforward. Items 4 and 5 follow from items 1, 2 and 3. By the Leibniz rule for the partial derivative of a product, we have</p>
    <me>
        [PQ,H] = (PQ)_x H_y - (PQ)_y H_x = (P_x Q + P Q_x) H_y - (P_y Q + P Q_y) H_x = [P,H]Q + P[Q,H],
    </me>
    <p>which proves that item 2 is true. Item 3 follows by straightforward induction using item 2.</p>
</proof>

<remark xml:id="P_Pm_Qn">
    <title></title>
    <p>Let <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> in <m>K[x,y]</m>. From items 1 and 5 of Lemma <xref ref="propiedades_basicas_del_jacobiano"/> it follow that</p>
    <me>
        [P, P^m] = m P^{m-1}[P,P] = 0 \quad\text{and}\quad [P, Q^n] = n Q^{n-1}[P,Q] \qquad\text{for all } m,n\in \mathbb{N}.
    </me>
    <p>Hence, by item 4 of Lemma <xref ref="propiedades_basicas_del_jacobiano"/>,</p>
    <me>
        [P, P^m Q^n] = [P,P^m] Q^n + P^m [P, Q^n] = P^m [P, Q^n] = \begin{cases} n P^m Q^{n-1}[P,Q] &amp; \text{if } n>0, \\ 0 &amp; \text{if } n=0. \end{cases}
    </me>
    <p>Using this it follows easily that if <m>[P,Q]\in K^{\times}</m>, then the set <m>\{P^i Q^j : i,j\in \mathbb{N}_0\}</m> is <m>K</m>-linearly independent.</p>
</remark>

<p>From now on we will use Lemma <xref ref="propiedades_basicas_del_jacobiano"/> without explicit mention.</p>

<example>
    <p>Let <m>P = x^4 y^2 + 2x^3 y^2 + x^2 y^2</m> and <m>Q = x^6 y^3 + 3x^5 y^3 + 3x^4 y^3 + x^3 y^3</m>. Then</p>
    <me>
        \begin{aligned}
        P_x &amp;= 4x^3 y^2 + 6x^2 y^2 + 2x y^2, &amp;&amp; Q_x = 6x^5 y^3 + 15x^4 y^3 + 12x^3 y^3 + 3x^2 y^3,\\
        P_y &amp;= 2x^4 y + 4x^3 y + 2x^2 y, &amp;&amp; Q_y = 3x^6 y^2 + 9x^5 y^2 + 9x^4 y^2 + 3x^3 y^2.
        \end{aligned}
    </me>
    <p>A straightforward computation shows that <m>[P,Q] = P_x Q_y - P_y Q_x = 0</m>. We can verify this equality faster, 
    if we note that for <m>R = xy(1+x)</m>, we have <m>P = R^2</m> and <m>Q = R^3</m>.
     Then, <m>[P,Q] = [R^2, R^3] = 6 R^3[R,R] = 0</m>. In general, if there exist 
     <m>R \in K[x,y]</m>, <m>\lambda_P, \lambda_Q \in K</m> and <m>m,n \in \mathbb{N}_0</m>,
      such that <m>P = \lambda_P R^m</m> and <m>Q = \lambda_Q R^n</m>, then <m>[P,Q] = 0</m>. 
      Under certain conditions the reciprocal is true, as we will see in Proposition <xref ref="propiedades_basicas_del_jacobiano"/>.</p>
</example>  // P_y_Q_alineados 

<exercises>
    <exercise>
        <p>Prove that if <m>[P,Q] \in K^{\times}</m>, then <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> are algebraically independent,
         or equivalently, that the set <m>\{P^i Q^j : i,j \in \mathbb{N}_0\}</m> is <m>K</m>-linearly independent.</p>
    </exercise>
</exercises>


  </section>

  <!-- ===================== -->
  <!-- SECTION 1.3 -->
  <!-- ===================== -->

  <section xml:id="sec-1-3">
    <title>Plane Jacobian conjecture and small examples</title>
    <p>Let <m>P,Q,R,S\in K[x,y]</m>. Since <m>f_{P,Q}\circ f_{R,S} = f_{P(R,S),Q(R,S)}</m>, the chain rule says that</p>

    <men xml:id="regla_de_la_cadena">
    \begin{pmatrix} P(R,S)_x &amp; P(R,S)_y \\ Q(R,S)_x &amp; Q(R,S)_y \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} P_x(R,S) &amp;
     P_y(R,S) \\ Q_x(R,S) &amp; Q_y(R,S) \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} R_x &amp; R_y \\ S_x &amp; S_y \end{pmatrix}.
    </men>
<p>Let <m>\varphi\colon K[x,y]\to K[x,y]</m> be an endomorphism of algebras and write <m>R := \varphi(x)</m> and <m>S :=
 \varphi(y)</m>. Then equality <xref ref="regla_de_la_cadena"/> becomes</p>
<me>
    \begin{pmatrix} \varphi(P)_x &amp; \varphi(P)_y \\ \varphi(Q)_x &amp; \varphi(Q)_y \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 
    \varphi(P_x) &amp; \varphi(P_y) \\ \varphi(Q_x) &amp; \varphi(Q_y) \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} \varphi(x)_x &amp; 
    \varphi(x)_y \\ \varphi(y)_x &amp; \varphi(y)_y \end{pmatrix},
</me>
<p>since <m>\varphi(P) = P(R,S)</m>, etcetera. Consequently</p>
<men xml:id="Jacobiano_de_una_composicion">
    [\varphi(P),\varphi(Q)] = \varphi([P,Q])[\varphi(x),\varphi(y)]\quad\text{for all } P,Q\in K[x,y].
</men>

<definition xml:id="inversa_polinomial">
    <title>Polynomial inverse</title>
    <p>Let <m>f = f_{P,Q}</m> be a polynomial map. We say that <term>f has a polynomial inverse</term> if <m>f</m> is invertible and there exist <m>R,S\in K[x,y]</m> such that <m>f^{-1} = f_{R,S}</m>.</p>
</definition>

<remark xml:id="cuando_es_iso_f_P_Q">
    <p>Clearly <m>f_{R,S}</m> is inverse of <m>f_{P,Q}</m> if and only if</p>
    <men xml:id="f_R_S_es_inversa_de_f_P_Q">
        R(P,Q) = P(R,S) = x \quad\text{and}\quad S(P,Q) = Q(R,S) = y
    </men>
</remark>

<remark xml:id="condicion_necesaria">
    <p>If <m>f = f_{P,Q}</m> has a polynomial inverse <m>f_{R,S}</m>, then, by <xref ref="regla_de_la_cadena"/></p>
    <me>
        \begin{pmatrix} R_x(P,Q) &amp; R_y(P,Q) \\ S_x(P,Q) &amp; S_y(P,Q) \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} P_x &amp; P_y \\ Q_x &amp; Q_y \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 1 \end{pmatrix},
    </me>
    <p>and so</p>
    <me>
        \operatorname{J}(R,S)|_{(P,Q)} \operatorname{J}(P,Q) = 1.
    </me>
    <p>Consequently <m>[P,Q] = \operatorname{J}(P,Q) \in K^{\times}</m>, since the only invertible polynomials are the non zero constants.</p>
</remark>

<p><title>Plane Jacobian conjecture (version 1)</title></p>
<p>The plane Jacobian conjecture states that if <m>[P,Q] \in K^{\times}</m>, then the polynomial map <m>f_{P,Q}\colon K^2 \to K^2</m> has a polynomial inverse. In other words, the Jacobian conjecture states that the necessary condition for the invertibility of <m>f_{P,Q}</m>, obtained above, is sufficient.</p>

<definition xml:id="par_jacobiano">
    <title>Jacobian pair</title>
    <p>A <term>Jacobian pair</term> is a pair <m>(P,Q)</m>, of polynomials <m>P,Q\in K[x,y]</m>, such that <m>[P,Q] \in K^{\times}</m>.</p>
</definition>

<p>For a pair of polynomials <m>(P,Q)</m> in two variables with coefficients in <m>K</m>, we let <m>K[P,Q]</m> and <m>\varphi_{P,Q}</m> denote the <m>K</m>-subalgebra of <m>K[x,y]</m> generated by <m>P,Q</m> and the <m>K</m>-algebra endomorphism of <m>K[x,y]</m> defined by <m>\varphi_{P,Q}(R) = R(P,Q)</m>, respectively.</p>

<proposition xml:id="equivalencia_de_que_f_PQ_sea_iso">
    <title></title>
    <p>Let <m>P,Q\in K[x,y]</m>. The following assertions are equivalent:</p>
    <ol>
        <li><m>f_{P,Q}</m> has a polynomial inverse.</li>
        <li><m>\varphi_{P,Q}</m> is an automorphism.</li>
        <li><m>K[P,Q] = K[x,y]</m>.</li>
    </ol>
</proposition>

<proof>
    <p>By definition</p>
    <me>
        \varphi_{R,S}\bigl(\varphi_{P,Q}(x)\bigr) = \varphi_{R,S}(P) = P(R,S) \quad\text{and}\quad \varphi_{R,S}\bigl(\varphi_{P,Q}(y)\bigr) = \varphi_{R,S}(Q) = Q(R,S).
    </me>
    <p>Therefore, <m>\varphi_{R,S} \circ \varphi_{P,Q} = \varphi_{P(R,S),Q(R,S)}</m>. Similarly <m>\varphi_{P,Q} \circ \varphi_{R,S} = \varphi_{R(P,Q),S(P,Q)}</m>, and so <m>\varphi_{R,S}</m> is the inverse of <m>\varphi_{P,Q}</m> if and only if identities <xref ref="f_R_S_es_inversa_de_f_P_Q"/> are fulfilled. This proves that items 1 and 2 are equivalent. Clearly, 2 implies 3. It remains to prove that 3 implies 2. But this is true, since 3 says that <m>\varphi_{P,Q}</m> is surjective, and each surjective algebra endomorphism of <m>K[x,y]</m> is an isomorphism, because <m>K[x,y]</m> is Noetherian.</p>
</proof>


    <p>
    By <xref ref="equivalencia_de_que_f_PQ_sea_iso"/>, version 1 of the Jacobian conjecture is equivalent to the following ones:
  </p>

  <p>
    <term>Plane Jacobian conjecture (version 2).</term> 
    If <m>[P,Q]\in K^{\times}</m>, then <m>\varphi_{P,Q}</m> is an automorphism of <m>K[x,y]</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    <term>Plane Jacobian conjecture (version 3).</term> 
    If <m>[P,Q]\in K^{\times}</m>, then <m>K[P,Q] = K[x,y]</m>.
  </p>

  <remark xml:id="automorfismos_elementales">
    <p>
      Clearly, for each polynomial in one variable <m>p</m> with coefficients in <m>K</m>, the maps 
      <m>\varphi_{x,y+p(x)}</m> and <m>\varphi_{x+p(y),y}</m> are invertible (with inverses 
      <m>\varphi_{x,y-p(x)}</m> and <m>\varphi_{x-p(y),y}</m>, respectively). These maps are called 
      <term>elementary automorphisms</term> <idx>elementary automorphisms</idx>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="un_ejemplo_de_varphi_inversible">
    <p>
      Let <m>P := x^4 + 2x^2y + y^2 + x</m> and <m>Q := x^2 + y</m>. Notice that
      <md>
        <mrow>
          [P,Q] &amp;= [x^4,x^2] + [2x^2y,x^2] + [y^2,x^2] + [x,x^2] + [x^4,y] + [2x^2y,y] + [y^2,y] + [x,y]
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          &amp;= 2x^2[y,x^2] + [y^2,x^2] + [x^4,y] + y[2x^2,y] + [x,y]
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          &amp;= -4x^3[x,y] - 4xy[x,y] + 4x^3[x,y] + 4xy[x,y] + [x,y]
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          &amp;= 1.
        </mrow>
      </md>
      As <m>\varphi_{P,Q} = \varphi_{x,x^2+y} \circ \varphi_{y^2+x,y}</m>, 
      <xref ref="automorfismos_elementales"/> implies that <m>\varphi_{P,Q}</m> is an automorphism.
    </p>
  </example>

  <proposition xml:id="P_Q_jac_pair_implica_varphi_P_varphi_Q_jac_pair">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <title>Jacobian pair under automorphism.</title>
        If <m>(P,Q)</m> is a Jacobian pair and <m>\varphi \in \operatorname{Aut}(K[x,y])</m>, then 
        <m>(\varphi(P),\varphi(Q))</m> also is a Jacobian pair.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </proposition>

  <proof>
    <p>
      By the equivalence between items 1 and 2 of <xref ref="equivalencia_de_que_f_PQ_sea_iso"/> and 
      <xref ref="condicion_necesaria"/>, we know that <m>[\varphi(x),\varphi(y)] \in K^{\times}</m>. 
      Hence, from <xref ref="Jacobiano_de_una_composicion"/>, we obtain 
      <m>[\varphi(P),\varphi(Q)] \in K^{\times}</m>.
    </p>
  </proof>

  <proposition xml:id="P_Q_contra_implica_varphi_P_varphi_Q_contra">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <title>Counterexample under automorphism.</title>
        If <m>(P,Q)</m> is a counterexample to the Jacobian Conjecture and 
        <m>\varphi \in \operatorname{Aut}(K[x,y])</m>, then <m>(\varphi(P),\varphi(Q))</m> also is a 
        counterexample to the Jacobian Conjecture.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </proposition>

  <proof>
    <p>
      By the previous proposition, <m>[\varphi(P),\varphi(Q)] \in K^{\times}</m>. Set 
      <m>R := \varphi(x)</m> and <m>S := \varphi(y)</m>, so that <m>\varphi = \varphi_{R,S}</m>. 
      Since <m>\varphi(P) = P(R,S)</m> and <m>\varphi(Q) = Q(R,S)</m>, we have
      <me>
        f_{\varphi(P),\varphi(Q)} = f_{P(R,S),Q(R,S)} = f_{P,Q} \circ f_{R,S}.
      </me>
      Moreover, again by <xref ref="equivalencia_de_que_f_PQ_sea_iso"/>, we know that <m>f_{R,S}</m> 
      has a polynomial inverse. Consequently <m>f_{\varphi(P),\varphi(Q)}</m> has a polynomial inverse 
      if and only if <m>f_{P,Q}</m> does.
    </p>
  </proof>

  <p>
    From now on we will use <xref ref="P_Q_contra_implica_varphi_P_varphi_Q_contra"/> without explicit mention.
  </p>

  <proposition xml:id="casos_pequenhos">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <title>Small degree cases.</title>
        Let <m>(P,Q)</m> be a Jacobian pair. If
        <me>
          P \in K[x],\quad P \in K[y],\quad Q \in K[x],\quad Q \in K[y]\quad\text{or}\quad \deg(P) + \deg(Q) \le 2,
        </me>
        then <m>\varphi_{P,Q}</m> is an automorphism.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </proposition>

  <proof>
    <p>
      Assume that <m>P \in K[x]</m>, which implies <m>P_y = 0</m>. Hence 
      <m>P_x Q_y = [P,Q] \in K^{\times}</m>. So, <m>P_x, Q_y \in K^{\times}</m> and consequently, 
      there exist <m>a \in K</m> and <m>b,c \in K^{\times}</m> such that 
      <m>P = a + bx</m> and <m>Q = cy + Q_0(x)</m>. Then, the polynomials
      <me>
        R := \frac{x - a}{b}\quad\text{and}\quad S := \frac{1}{c}\left(y - Q_0\!\left(\frac{x-a}{b}\right)\right)
      </me>
      satisfy the conditions <m>R(P,Q) = x</m>, <m>S(P,Q) = y</m>, <m>P(R,S) = x</m> and 
      <m>Q(R,S) = y</m>; and consequently, <m>\varphi_{P,Q}</m> is an automorphism. A similar argument shows 
      that a Jacobian pair with <m>P \in K[y]</m>, <m>Q \in K[x]</m> or <m>Q \in K[y]</m> defines an automorphism. 
      It remains to prove that if <m>\deg(P) + \deg(Q) \le 2</m>, then <m>\varphi_{P,Q}</m> is also an automorphism. 
      If <m>\deg(P) = 0</m> or <m>\deg(Q) = 0</m>, then <m>[P,Q] = 0</m>, which contradicts the fact that 
      <m>(P,Q)</m> is a Jacobian pair. Thus, necessarily <m>\deg(P) = \deg(Q) = 1</m>, and so there exist 
      <m>a,b,c,d,e,f \in K</m> such that <m>(b,c) \ne (0,0)</m>, <m>(e,f) \ne (0,0)</m>,
      <me>
        P = a + bx + cy\quad\text{and}\quad Q = d + ex + fy.
      </me>
      Moreover, <m>bf - ec = \bigl| \begin{smallmatrix}b &amp; c \\ e &amp; f \end{smallmatrix}\bigr| = [P,Q] \ne 0</m>. Let
      <me>
        R := \frac{1}{[P,Q]}(cd - af + fx - cy)\quad\text{and}\quad 
        S := \frac{1}{[P,Q]}(ae - bd - ex + by).
      </me>
      A straightforward computation shows that <m>R(P,Q) = x</m>, <m>S(P,Q) = y</m>, <m>P(R,S) = x</m> and 
      <m>Q(R,S) = y</m>, and thus <m>\varphi_{P,Q}</m> is an automorphism.
    </p>
  </proof>

  <p>
    A technique we will frequently use in these notes is the <q>reduction</q> of the support. Given a pair 
    <m>(P,Q)</m> of polynomials in <m>K[x,y]</m> and a problem involving <m>(P,Q)</m>, the idea is to apply an 
    automorphism <m>\varphi</m> of <m>K[x,y]</m> such that:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Solving the problem for <m>(\varphi(P),\varphi(Q))</m> implies solving it for <m>(P,Q)</m>,</li>
    <li>the supports of <m>\varphi(P)</m> and <m>\varphi(Q)</m> make the problem easier to solve for these polynomials.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Many times the modification of the support involves a true reduction of the size of the supports of 
    <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> in some sense (cardinal, area, etc.). For instance, suppose that 
    <m>\varphi_{P,Q}</m> is invertible and that we want to find <m>\varphi_{P,Q}^{-1}</m>. Since
    <me>
      \varphi_{P,Q}^{-1}(P) = x\quad\text{and}\quad \varphi_{P,Q}^{-1}(Q) = y,
    </me>
    an idea is to take an automorphism <m>\varphi_1</m> that reduces the supports of <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m>; 
    then, an automorphism <m>\varphi_2</m> that reduces the supports of <m>\varphi_1(P)</m> and 
    <m>\varphi_1(Q)</m>; and so on. Let us see how this technique works in a concrete case: take 
    <m>P := x^4 + 2x^2y + y^2 + x</m> and <m>Q := x^2 + y</m>. We saw in 
    <xref ref="un_ejemplo_de_varphi_inversible"/> that <m>\varphi_{P,Q}</m> is invertible. In order to find 
    <m>\varphi_{P,Q}^{-1}</m>, we first apply the elementary automorphism 
    <m>\varphi_1 := \varphi_{x,y-x^2}</m>, and we obtain <m>\varphi_1(P) = x + y^2</m> and 
    <m>\varphi_1(Q) = y</m> (see <xref ref="fig_10"/>).
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig_10">
    <caption>Reduction of the support of <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> by <m>\varphi_1</m></caption>
    <image width="90%" source="img/img10.png"/>
  </figure>
  <p>
  Then, we apply the elementary automorphism <m>\varphi_2 := \varphi_{x-y^2,y}</m>. Clearly
  <me>
    \varphi_2(\varphi_1(P)) = x \quad\text{and}\quad \varphi_2(\varphi_1(Q)) = y,
  </me>
  and so <m>\varphi_2 \circ \varphi_1 = \varphi_{P,Q}^{-1}</m>. By the way, it is also possible to find 
  <m>\varphi_{P,Q}^{-1}</m> using <xref ref="automorfismos_elementales"/> and 
  <xref ref="un_ejemplo_de_varphi_inversible"/>.
</p>

<p>
  Later we will also use the support reduction technique in some extensions of the polynomial ring in two variables.
</p>

<subsection xml:id="ejercicios">
  <title>Exercises</title>

  <exercise xml:id="ejercicio_1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that if <m>A</m> is a Noetherian ring, then every surjective endomorphism of <m>A</m> is an automorphism.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ejercicio_2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that if <m>Q_y \in K</m>, then there exist <m>c \in K</m> and <m>Q_0 \in K[x]</m> such that 
        <m>Q = cy + Q_0(x)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
</subsection>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== -->
  <!-- SECTION 1.4 -->
  <!-- ===================== -->

  <section xml:id="sec-1-4">
    <title>Directions, leading terms, starting points and end points</title>
   <p>
    We denote by <m>\mathfrak{V}</m> the set of all <term>directions</term> <idx>directions</idx> in <m>\mathbb{Z}^2</m>, 
    which by definition is the set of all <m>(\rho,\sigma)\in \mathbb{Z}^2\setminus \{(0,0)\}</m> such that 
    <m>\gcd(\rho,\sigma) = 1</m>. We also denote by <m>\mathfrak{V}_{\ge 0}</m> and <m>\mathfrak{V}_{&gt;0}</m>, the sets
    <me>
      \mathfrak{V}_{\ge 0} := \{(\rho,\sigma)\in \mathfrak{V} : \rho+\sigma \ge 0\}
      \quad\text{and}\quad
      \mathfrak{V}_{&gt;0} := \{(\rho,\sigma)\in \mathfrak{V} : \rho+\sigma &gt; 0\},
    </me>
    of <term>non negative</term> <idx>directions!non negative</idx> and <term>positive</term> 
    <idx>directions!positive</idx> directions, respectively.
  </p>

  <definition xml:id="definicion_de_v">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <title>Valuation of a monomial.</title>
        For all <m>(\rho,\sigma)\in \mathfrak{V}</m> and <m>(i,j)\in \mathbb{N}_0 \times \mathbb{N}_0</m>, we write 
        <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(i,j) := \rho i + \sigma j</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <definition xml:id="valuaciones_para_polinomios">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <title>Valuation of a polynomial.</title>
        Let <m>(\rho,\sigma)\in \mathfrak{V}</m>. For <m>P := \sum a_{ij} x^i y^j \in K[x,y]\setminus\{0\}</m>, we define:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            The <term><m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-degree</term> 
            <idx><m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-degree</idx> of <m>P</m> as 
            <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(P) := \max\{ v_{\rho\sigma}(i,j) : a_{ij} \ne 0\}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The <term><m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-leading term</term> 
            <idx><m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-leading term</idx> of <m>P</m> as 
            <m>\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P) := \displaystyle \sum_{\rho i+\sigma j = v_{\rho\sigma}(P)} a_{ij} x^i y^j</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Note that the usual <term>total degree</term> <idx>total degree</idx> of <m>P</m> is 
        <m>\deg(P) := v_{11}(P)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <note xml:id="valuacion_de_cero">
    <p>
      To abbreviate expressions we set <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(0) := -\infty</m> and <m>\ell_{\rho\sigma}(0) := 0</m>, 
      for all <m>(\rho,\sigma)\in \mathfrak{V}</m>.
    </p>
  </note>

  <p>
    In <xref ref="fig_11"/> are drawn <m>\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m>, <m>\ell_{\rho\sigma}(Q)</m> and 
    <m>\ell_{\rho\sigma}(PQ)</m> for some directions <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m> and
    <me>
      (P,Q) = (x^3y + x^2y^2 + xy^2 + x^2 + y + 2,\; 4xy^2 - y^2 + 2x - 1).
    </me>
    The representation of <m>\ell_{\rho\sigma}(PQ)</m> illustrates 
    <xref ref="pr_v_de_un_producto1"/> (1).
  </p>

<figure xml:id="fig_11">
        <image width="100%" source="img/img11.png"/>
       <caption>Examples of <m>\ell_{\rho\sigma}</m></caption>
  </figure>



  <definition xml:id="elementos_rho_sigma_homogeneos">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <title><m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous polynomial.</title>
        We say that <m>P \in K[x,y]</m> is <term><m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous</term> 
        <idx><m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous</idx> if <m>P = \ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <remark xml:id="euler">
    <p>
      Since
      <me>
        \rho x \frac{\partial x^i y^j}{\partial x} + \sigma y \frac{\partial x^i y^j}{\partial y} 
        = (\rho i + \sigma j) x^i y^j = v_{\rho\sigma}(x^i y^j) x^i y^j,
      </me>
      a polynomial <m>P \ne 0</m> is <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous with <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(P) = \tau</m> 
      if and only if <m>\rho x P_x + \sigma y P_y = \tau P</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <p>
    For all <m>P \in K[x,y] \setminus \{0\}</m>, each one of the edges of <m>\operatorname{HN}(P)</m> is the convex hull 
    of the support of <m>\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m>, where <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m> is orthogonal to the given edge and 
    points outside of <m>\operatorname{HN}(P)</m>.
  </p>

  <remark xml:id="pr_v_de_un_producto1">
    <p>
      Let <m>P,Q \in K[x,y]\setminus\{0\}</m> and <m>(\rho,\sigma) \in \mathfrak{V}</m>. The following assertions hold:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><m>\ell_{\rho\sigma}(PQ) = \ell_{\rho\sigma}(P) \ell_{\rho\sigma}(Q)</m>.</li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Let <m>P = \sum_i P_i</m>. If there exists <m>\tau \in \mathbb{Z}</m> such that 
          <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(P_i) = \tau</m>, for all <m>i</m>, and <m>\sum_i \ell_{\rho\sigma}(P_i) \ne 0</m>, then
          <me>
            \ell_{\rho\sigma}(P) = \sum_i \ell_{\rho\sigma}(P_i) \quad\text{and}\quad v_{\rho\sigma}(P) = \tau.
          </me>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          If <m>P = P_1 + P_2 + \cdots + P_r</m> and <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(P_1) &gt; v_{\rho\sigma}(P_2) &gt; \cdots</m>, 
          then <m>\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P) = \ell_{\rho\sigma}(P_1)</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li><m>v_{\rho\sigma}(PQ) = v_{\rho\sigma}(P) + v_{\rho\sigma}(Q)</m>.</li>
      <li><m>-v_{-\rho,-\sigma}(P) \le v_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Frequently, we will use these facts throughout the book without explicit mention.
    </p>
  </remark> 
  <proposition xml:id="suma_de_Hs">
  <statement>
    <p>
      <title>Newton polygon of a product.</title>
      The identity <m>\operatorname{HN}(PQ) = \operatorname{HN}(P) + \operatorname{HN}(Q)</m> holds for each 
      <m>P,Q \in K[x,y]\setminus\{0\}</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</proposition>

<proof>
  <p>
    For each direction <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>, let 
    <m>s_{\rho\sigma}(PQ) := \{(i,j) \in \mathbb{Z}^2 : v_{\rho\sigma}(i,j) \le v_{\rho\sigma}(PQ)\}</m>. 
    By the discussion below <xref ref="poligono_de_Newton"/>, we know that 
    <m>\operatorname{HN}(PQ) \subseteq \operatorname{HN}(P) + \operatorname{HN}(Q)</m>. The opposite inclusion 
    follows from the fact that <m>\operatorname{HN}(PQ) = \bigcap_{(\rho,\sigma)\in \mathfrak{V}} s_{\rho\sigma}(PQ)</m> 
    and <m>\operatorname{HN}(P) + \operatorname{HN}(Q) \subseteq s_{\rho\sigma}(PQ)</m> by 
    <xref ref="pr_v_de_un_producto1"/>(4).
  </p>
</proof>

<p>
  Let <m>P \in K[x,y]</m> and let <m>(\rho,\sigma) \in \mathfrak{V}</m>. We say that <m>P = \sum_i P_i</m> is the 
  <term><m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous decomposition of <m>P</m></term> 
  <idx><m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous decomposition</idx> if <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(P_i) = i</m>, for all <m>P_i \ne 0</m>.
</p>

<remark xml:id="re_v_de_un_conmutador">
  <p>
    Let <m>P,Q \in K[x,y]\setminus\{0\}</m> and let <m>(\rho,\sigma) \in \mathfrak{V}</m>. We have:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <p>
        If <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> are <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous, then <m>[P,Q]</m> is also. Moreover, 
        if <m>[P,Q] \ne 0</m>, then
        <me>
          v_{\rho\sigma}([P,Q]) = v_{\rho\sigma}(P) + v_{\rho\sigma}(Q) - (\rho+\sigma).
        </me>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        If <m>P = \sum_i P_i</m> and <m>Q = \sum_j Q_j</m> are the <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous decompositions 
        of <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m>, then the <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous decomposition 
        <m>[P,Q] = \sum_k [P,Q]_k</m> is given by
        <men xml:id="descomposicion">
          [P,Q]_k = \sum_{i+j = k + \rho + \sigma} [P_i, Q_j].
        </men>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>If <m>[P,Q] = 0</m>, then <m>[\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P), \ell_{\rho\sigma}(Q)] = 0</m>.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</remark>

<proposition xml:id="pr_v_de_un_conmutador">
  <statement>
    <p>
      <title>Valuation of the Jacobian.</title>
      Let <m>P,Q \in K[x,y]\setminus \{0\}</m>. Then
      <me xml:id="dfpvc">
        v_{\rho\sigma}([P,Q]) \le v_{\rho\sigma}(P) + v_{\rho\sigma}(Q) - (\rho+\sigma),
      </me>
      for each <m>(\rho,\sigma) \in \mathfrak{V}</m>. Moreover,
      <me>
        v_{\rho\sigma}([P,Q]) = v_{\rho\sigma}(P) + v_{\rho\sigma}(Q) - (\rho+\sigma) 
        \iff [\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P), \ell_{\rho\sigma}(Q)] \ne 0,
      </me>
      and, in this case, <m>[\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P), \ell_{\rho\sigma}(Q)] = \ell_{\rho\sigma}([P,Q])</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</proposition>

<proof>
  <p>It follows directly from the decomposition <xref ref="descomposicion"/>.</p>
</proof>

<note  xml:id="Comienzo_y_Fin_de_un_elemento_de_W">
  <p>
    Let <m>(\rho,\sigma) \in \mathfrak{V}</m> arbitrary and let <m>P \in K[x,y]\setminus \{0\}</m>. We let 
    <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m> and <m>\operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m> denote the first and the last point that we find on 
    <m>H(\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P))</m> when we run counterclockwise along the boundary of <m>H(P)</m>. The identities in 
    <xref ref="eq_st_y_en"/> can be taken as a formal definition of the <term>starting point 
    <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m></term> <idx>starting point</idx> and the <term>end point 
    <m>\operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m></term> <idx>end point</idx> of <m>\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m>. Note that these points 
    coincide if and only if <m>\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m> is a monomial.
  </p>
</note >

<remark xml:id="st_y_en">
  <p>
    The identities
    <me>
      \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(PQ) = \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P) + \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(Q)
      \quad\text{and}\quad
      \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(PQ) = \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(P) + \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(Q)
    </me>
    hold for each <m>P,Q \in K[x,y]\setminus\{0\}</m> and <m>(\rho,\sigma) \in \mathfrak{V}</m>. Moreover, we have
    <men xml:id="eq_st_y_en">
      \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P) = \operatorname{Supp}(\ell_{\sigma,-\rho}(\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)))
      \quad\text{and}\quad
      \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(P) = \operatorname{Supp}(\ell_{-\sigma,\rho}(\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P))),
    </men>
    as is illustrated in <xref ref="fig_12"/>.
  </p>
</remark>

<figure xml:id="fig_12">
  <caption>The starting and end point of <m>\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m></caption>
  <image width="75%" source="img/img12.png"/>
</figure>

<remark xml:id="acerca_del_flip">
  <p>
    Let <m>P \in K[x,y]</m>, <m>(\rho,\sigma) \in \mathfrak{V}</m> and let <m>\tau := \varphi_{y,x}</m> be the 
    automorphism of <m>K[x,y]</m> that interchanges <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>. A direct computation shows that 
    <m>\ell_{\rho\sigma}(\tau(P)) = \tau(\ell_{\sigma\rho}(P))</m>. From this and equalities 
    <xref ref="eq_st_y_en"/> it follows that <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(\tau(P)) = \overline{\tau}(\operatorname{en}_{\sigma\rho}(P))</m> 
    and <m>\operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(\tau(P)) = \overline{\tau}(\operatorname{stn}_{\sigma\rho}(P))</m>, where <m>\overline{\tau}</m> 
    denotes the flip of <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>.
  </p>
</remark>

<p>
  The <term>cross product</term> <idx>cross product</idx> of two vectors <m>A = (a_1,a_2)</m> and 
  <m>B = (b_1,b_2)</m> in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m> is 
  <m>A \times B := \det\begin{pmatrix} a_1 &amp; a_2 \\ b_1 &amp; b_2 \end{pmatrix}</m>.
</p>

<remark xml:id="starting_and_end_with_cross">
  <p>
    If <m>\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m> is not a monomial, then 
    <m>(\rho,\sigma) \times (\operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(P) - \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P)) &gt; 0</m>.
  </p>
</remark>

<remark xml:id="polinomio_asociado_f">
  <p>
    Let <m>P \in K[x,y]\setminus\{0\}</m> and <m>(\rho,\sigma) \in \mathfrak{V}</m>. Set 
    <m>z := x^{-\sigma} y^{\rho} \in K[x,y,x^{-1},y^{-1}]</m> and write
    <me>
      \ell_{\rho\sigma}(P) = x^r y^s p(z),
    </me>
    where <m>p := \sum_{i=0}^{\gamma} a_i z^i \in K[z]</m> with <m>a_0 \ne 0</m> and <m>a_{\gamma} \ne 0</m>. Since 
    <m>v_{\sigma,-\rho}(z) = -\rho^2 - \sigma^2 &lt; 0</m>, by <xref ref="eq_st_y_en"/> we have
    <men xml:id="calculo_de_st_y_en">
      \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P) = (r,s) \quad\text{and}\quad
      \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(P) = (r - \gamma\sigma,\; s + \gamma\rho).
    </men>
  </p>
</remark>

<definition xml:id="def_vectors_alineados">
  <statement>
    <p>
      <title>Aligned vectors.</title>
      For two vectors <m>A,B</m> in any <m>K</m>-vector space, we write <m>A \sim B</m>, if there exists 
      <m>\lambda \in K^{\times}</m> such that <m>A = \lambda B</m>. In this case we say that <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> 
      <term>are aligned</term> <idx>aligned vectors</idx>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>

<remark>
  <p>
    Note that, for <m>A,B \in \mathbb{R}^2</m>,
    <me>
      A \times B = 0 \iff A = 0,\; B = 0 \text{ or } A \sim B.
    </me>
    So,
    <me>
      A \sim B \iff A = B = 0,\; \text{or } A \ne 0,\; B \ne 0 \text{ and } A \times B = 0.
    </me>
  </p>
</remark>

<remark xml:id="Jacobian_pair_como_alineados">
  <p>
    If <m>P,Q \in K[x,y]</m>, then <m>[P,Q] \sim 1</m> means that <m>[P,Q] \in K^{\times}</m>, and so 
    <m>(P,Q)</m> is a Jacobian pair.
  </p>
</remark>

<proposition xml:id="extremos_alineados">
  <statement>
    <p>
      <title>Extremal points of aligned Jacobian.</title>
      Let <m>P,Q \in K[x,y]\setminus \{0\}</m> and <m>(\rho,\sigma) \in \mathfrak{V}</m>. If 
      <m>[\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P), \ell_{\rho\sigma}(Q)] = 0</m>, then
      <me>
        \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P) \times \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(Q) = 0
        \quad\text{and}\quad
        \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(P) \times \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(Q) = 0.
      </me>
    </p>
  </statement>
</proposition>

<proof>
  <p>
    Write <m>(r,s) := \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m> and <m>(u,v) := \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(Q)</m>. By identities 
    <xref ref="eq_st_y_en"/> there exist <m>\lambda_p, \lambda_q \in K^{\times}</m> such that
    <me>
      \ell_{\sigma,-\rho}(\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)) = \lambda_p x^r y^s
      \quad\text{and}\quad
      \ell_{\sigma,-\rho}(\ell_{\rho\sigma}(Q)) = \lambda_q x^u y^v.
    </me>
    Hence, by <xref ref="re_v_de_un_conmutador"/>(3),
    <me>
      (rv - su) \lambda_p \lambda_q x^{r+u-1} y^{s+v-1}
      = \bigl[\lambda_p x^r y^s, \lambda_q x^u y^v\bigr]
      = \bigl[\ell_{\sigma,-\rho}(\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)), \ell_{\sigma,-\rho}(\ell_{\rho\sigma}(Q))\bigr] = 0.
    </me>
    Thus, <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P) \times \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(Q) = rv - su = 0</m>. A similar argument shows that 
    <m>\operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(P) \times \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(Q) = 0</m>.
  </p>
</proof>

<proposition xml:id="extremos_no_alineados">
  <statement>
    <p>
      <title>Non-aligned extremal points.</title>
      Let <m>(\rho,\sigma) \in \mathfrak{V}</m> and let <m>P,Q,R \in K[x,y]\setminus \{0\}</m> be such that
      <me>
        [\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P), \ell_{\rho\sigma}(Q)] = \ell_{\rho\sigma}(R).
      </me>
      The following assertions hold:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P) \times \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(Q) \ne 0</m> if and only if 
          <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P) + \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(Q) - (1,1) = \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(R)</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>\operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(P) \times \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(Q) \ne 0</m> if and only if 
          <m>\operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(P) + \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(Q) - (1,1) = \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(R)</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </statement>
</proposition>
    
<proof>
  <p>
    We only prove item 1, since item 2 is similar. By replacing <m>P</m>, <m>Q</m> and <m>R</m> by 
    <m>\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m>, <m>\ell_{\rho\sigma}(Q)</m> and <m>\ell_{\rho\sigma}(R)</m> respectively, 
    we can assume that <m>P</m>, <m>Q</m> and <m>R</m> are <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous, and we do it. 
    Let <m>(r,s) := \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m>, <m>(u,v) := \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(Q)</m> and 
    <m>(m,n) := \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(R)</m>. By identity <xref ref="eq_st_y_en"/> there exist 
    <m>\lambda_P, \lambda_Q, \lambda_R \in K^{\times}</m> such that
    <men xml:id="starting">
      \ell_{\sigma,-\rho}(P) = \lambda_P x^r y^s,\quad
      \ell_{\sigma,-\rho}(Q) = \lambda_Q x^u y^v \quad\text{and}\quad
      \ell_{\sigma,-\rho}(R) = \lambda_R x^m y^n.
    </men>
    Clearly
    <me>
      [\ell_{\sigma,-\rho}(P), \ell_{\sigma,-\rho}(Q)] = (rv - su) \lambda_P \lambda_Q x^{r+u-1} y^{s+v-1},
    </me>
    and hence,
    <men xml:id="chvh4">
      \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P) \times \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(Q) \ne 0 \iff 
      [\ell_{\sigma,-\rho}(P), \ell_{\sigma,-\rho}(Q)] \ne 0.
    </men>
    Consequently, by <xref ref="pr_v_de_un_conmutador"/>, if 
    <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P) \times \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(Q) \ne 0</m>, then
    <me>
      \lambda_R x^m y^n = \ell_{\sigma,-\rho}(R) 
      = [\ell_{\sigma,-\rho}(P), \ell_{\sigma,-\rho}(Q)]
      = (rv - su) \lambda_P \lambda_Q x^{r+u-1} y^{s+v-1},
    </me>
    which implies that
    <me>
      \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P) + \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(Q) - (1,1) = \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(R),
    </me>
    as desired. Conversely, if the last equality holds, then, by the first identity in 
    <xref ref="eq_st_y_en"/>,
    <me>
      v_{\sigma,-\rho}(P) + v_{\sigma,-\rho}(Q) - (\sigma - \rho) = v_{\sigma,-\rho}(R),
    </me>
    and so, again by <xref ref="pr_v_de_un_conmutador"/>,
    <me>
      [\ell_{\sigma,-\rho}(P), \ell_{\sigma,-\rho}(Q)] \ne 0,
    </me>
    which by <xref ref="chvh4"/> implies that 
    <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P) \times \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(Q) \ne 0</m>.
  </p>
</proof>

<definition xml:id="homogeneous_endomorphism">
  <statement>
    <p>
      <title><m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous endomorphism.</title>
      Let <m>\varphi</m> be an injective endomorphism of <m>K[x,y]</m> and let <m>(\rho,\sigma) \in \mathfrak{V}</m>. 
      We say that <m>\varphi</m> is <term><m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous</term> 
      <idx><m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous injective endomorphism</idx> if, for all 
      <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous polynomial <m>P</m>,
      <me>
        \varphi(P) \text{ is } (\rho,\sigma)\text{-homogeneous and } 
        v_{\rho\sigma}(\varphi(P)) = v_{\rho\sigma}(P).
      </me>
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>

<remark>
  <p>
    By <xref ref="pr_v_de_un_producto1"/>, an injective endomorphism <m>\varphi</m> of <m>K[x,y]</m> is 
    <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous if and only if <m>\varphi(x)</m> and <m>\varphi(y)</m> are 
    <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous, <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(\varphi(x)) = \rho</m> and 
    <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(\varphi(y)) = \sigma</m>. From this it follows immediately that <m>\varphi</m> is 
    <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous if and only if it is <m>(-\rho,-\sigma)</m>-homogeneous.
  </p>
</remark>

<example xml:id="ejemplo_de_auto_homo">
  <p>
    For all <m>k \in \mathbb{N}_0</m> and <m>\lambda \in K</m>, the elementary automorphism 
    <m>\varphi_{x,y+\lambda x^k}</m> is <m>(1,k)</m>-homogeneous.
  </p>
</example>

<subsection xml:id="ejercicios_capitulo1">
  <title>Exercises</title>

  <exercise xml:id="ejercicio_pr_v_de_un_producto1">
    <statement>
      <p>Prove <xref ref="pr_v_de_un_producto1"/>.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ejercicio_re_v_de_un_conmutador">
    <statement>
      <p>Prove <xref ref="re_v_de_un_conmutador"/>.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ejercicio_st_y_en">
    <statement>
      <p>Prove <xref ref="st_y_en"/>.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ejercicio_starting_and_end_with_cross">
    <statement>
      <p>Prove <xref ref="starting_and_end_with_cross"/>.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</subsection>
    
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== -->
  <!-- SECTION 1.5 -->
  <!-- ===================== -->

  <section xml:id="sec-1-5">
    <title>The element F</title>
    
    <proposition xml:id="P_y_Q_alineados">
  <statement>
    <p>
      <title>Homogeneous Jacobian pair.</title>
      Let <m>(\rho,\sigma)\in \mathfrak{V}</m> and let <m>P,Q\in K[x,y]\setminus\{0\}</m> be two 
      <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous elements. Set <m>\tau := v_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m> and <m>\mu := v_{\rho\sigma}(Q)</m>.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>If <m>\tau = \mu = 0</m>, then <m>[P,Q] = 0</m>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Assume that <m>(\mu,\tau) \ne (0,0)</m> and let <m>m,n \in \mathbb{Z}</m> coprime such that 
          <m>n\tau = m\mu</m>. If <m>[P,Q] = 0</m>, then <m>\mu\tau \ge 0</m>, we can take <m>n,m \ge 0</m> 
          and the following facts hold:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>There exists <m>\alpha \in K^{\times}</m> such that <m>P^n = \alpha Q^m</m>.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              There exists a <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous polynomial <m>R</m>, such that 
              <m>P \sim R^m</m> and <m>Q \sim R^n</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </statement>
</proposition>

<proof>
  <p>
    <bold>1.</bold> For <m>(i,j) \in \mathbb{N}_0^2</m>, set 
    <m>\mathbb{N}_0(i,j) := \{\lambda (i,j) : \lambda \in \mathbb{N}_0\}</m>. Since <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> are 
    <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous and <m>\tau = \mu = 0</m>, there exists <m>(i,j) \ne (0,0)</m>, such that 
    <m>\operatorname{Supp}(P) \cup \operatorname{Supp}(Q) \subseteq \mathbb{N}_0(i,j)</m>. So 
    <m>P,Q \in K[z]</m>, where <m>z := x^i y^j</m>. From this and 
    <xref ref="propiedades_basicas_del_jacobiano"/>(1) it follows that <m>[P,Q] = 0</m>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <bold>2.</bold> To prove that <m>\mu\tau \ge 0</m>, we can assume that <m>\mu \ne 0</m> and <m>\tau \ne 0</m>, 
    which implies that <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P) \ne (0,0)</m> and 
    <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(Q) \ne (0,0)</m>. By <xref ref="extremos_alineados"/>, we know that 
    <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P) \times \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(Q) = 0</m>. So, there exists 
    <m>\lambda \ne 0</m> such that <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(Q) = \lambda \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m>. 
    Since both, <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m> and <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(Q)</m> are in 
    <m>\mathbb{N}_0^2</m>, necessarily <m>\lambda &gt; 0</m>. Therefore, 
    <m>\mu\tau = \lambda\tau^2 &gt; 0</m> and we can take <m>n,m \ge 0</m>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <bold>2a)</bold> By <xref ref="euler"/>, we have
    <me>
      \tau P Q_y - \mu Q P_y = (\rho x P_x + \sigma y P_y) Q_y - (\rho x Q_x + \sigma y Q_y) P_y
      = \rho x (P_x Q_y - P_y Q_x) = \rho x [P,Q] = 0.
    </me>
    Consequently <m>n Q P_y - m P Q_y = 0</m>. Thus,
    <me>
      \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(\frac{P^n}{Q^m}\right)
      = \frac{n P^{n-1} P_y Q^m - m Q^{m-1} Q_y P^n}{Q^{2m}}
      = \frac{P^{n-1} Q^{m-1}}{Q^{2m}} (n Q P_y - m P Q_y) = 0,
    </me>
    and similarly, <m>\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\bigl(\frac{P^n}{Q^m}\bigr) = 0</m>. Hence, 
    <m>\frac{P^n}{Q^m} = \alpha \in K^{\times}</m>, from which <m>P^n = \alpha Q^m</m> follows.
  </p>
  <p>
    <bold>2b)</bold> Set <m>(r,s) := \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m>, 
    <m>(u,v) := \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(Q)</m> and <m>z := x^{-\sigma} y^{\rho}</m>. Since <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> 
    are <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous and <m>v_{-\sigma,\rho}(z) = -\rho^2 - \sigma^2 &lt; 0</m>, it follows from 
    <xref ref="eq_st_y_en"/>, that
    <men xml:id="P_Q_y_z">
      P = x^r y^s f(z) \quad\text{and}\quad Q = x^u y^v g(z),
    </men>
    for some <m>f(z), g(z) \in K[z]</m> such that <m>f(0) \ne 0</m> and <m>g(0) \ne 0</m>. By statement 2a), there exists 
    <m>\alpha \in K^{\times}</m> such that
    <me>
      x^{nr} y^{ns} f^n(z) = P^n = \alpha Q^m = \alpha x^{mu} y^{mv} g^m(z),
    </me>
    which implies
    <men xml:id="nr_ns_mu_mv">
      (nr, ns) = (mu, mv) \quad\text{and}\quad f^n = \alpha g^m.
    </men>
    Since <m>\gcd(m,n) = 1</m>, by the second equality there exists <m>h \in K[z]</m> such that
    <men xml:id="chvh1">
      f \sim h^m \quad\text{and}\quad g \sim h^n.
    </men>
    Take <m>c,d \in \mathbb{Z}</m> such that <m>cm + dn = 1</m> and define 
    <m>R(x,y) := x^a y^b h(z)</m>, where <m>(a,b) := c(r,s) + d(u,v)</m>. Since, by the first identity in 
    <xref ref="nr_ns_mu_mv"/>,
    <men xml:id="ejercicio_mdc">
      m(a,b) = (r,s) \quad\text{and}\quad n(a,b) = (u,v),
    </men>
    from <xref ref="P_Q_y_z"/> and <xref ref="chvh1"/>, it follows that <m>P \sim R^m</m> and <m>Q \sim R^n</m>.
  </p>
</proof>

<remark xml:id="R_reducido">
  <p>
    A polynomial <m>R \in K[x,y]\setminus K</m> is said to be <term>not a proper power</term> 
    <idx>polynomial that is not a proper power</idx> if <m>R \sim S^i</m> implies <m>i = 1</m>. For every polynomial 
    <m>P \in K[x,y]\setminus K</m>, there exists a polynomial <m>R</m>, which is not a proper power, and a natural 
    number <m>n</m> such that <m>P \sim R^n</m>. Furthermore, if <m>P \sim S^m</m> for some <m>S</m>, then 
    <m>m \mid n</m>; and the following conditions are equivalent:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><m>S</m> is not a proper power,</li>
    <li><m>R \sim S</m>,</li>
    <li><m>m = n</m>,</li>
    <li>
      <m>m</m> is the largest integer for which there exists <m>S \in K[x,y]</m> such that <m>P \sim S^m</m>.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Clearly, in <xref ref="P_y_Q_alineados"/>(2b) we can take <m>R</m> not a proper power, but then we lose the 
    property that <m>\gcd(m,n) = 1</m>.
  </p>
</remark>

<lemma xml:id="Lema_Joseph">
  <statement>
    <p>
      <title>Joseph's Lemma.</title>
      Let <m>(\rho,\sigma) \in \mathfrak{V}</m> and let <m>(P,Q)</m> be a Jacobian pair. If 
      <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(P) \ne 0</m>, then there exists <m>G_0 \in K[P,Q]</m> such that
      <me>
        [\ell_{\rho\sigma}(G_0), \ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)] \ne 0
        \quad\text{and}\quad
        [[\ell_{\rho\sigma}(G_0), \ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)], \ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)] = 0.
      </me>
      Moreover, if we define recursively <m>G_i := [G_{i-1}, P]</m>, then 
      <m>[\ell_{\rho\sigma}(G_i), \ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)] = 0</m>, for <m>i \ge 1</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</lemma>

<proof>
  <p>
    For <m>t \in \mathbb{N}</m>, set
    <me>
      \mathfrak{M}(t) := \operatorname{linspan} \{P^i Q^j : 0 \le i,j \le t\}.
    </me>
    Since <m>\{P^i Q^j : 0 \le i,j \le t\}</m> is linearly independent (by <xref ref="P_Pm_Qn"/>), we have
    <men xml:id="chvh2">
      \dim \mathfrak{M}(t) = (t+1)^2.
    </men>
    On the other hand, by items 4 and 5 of <xref ref="pr_v_de_un_producto1"/>, we know that
    <me>
      mt \le -v_{-\rho,-\sigma}(z) \le v_{\rho\sigma}(z) \le Mt \quad\text{for each } z \in \mathfrak{M}(t) \setminus \{0\},
    </me>
    where
    <md>
      <mrow>
        m &amp;:= \min\{0, -v_{-\rho,-\sigma}(P), -v_{-\rho,-\sigma}(Q), -v_{-\rho,-\sigma}(P) - v_{-\rho,-\sigma}(Q)\}
      </mrow>
      <intertext>and</intertext>
      <mrow>
        M &amp;:= \max\{0, v_{\rho\sigma}(P), v_{\rho\sigma}(Q), v_{\rho\sigma}(P) + v_{\rho\sigma}(Q)\}.
      </mrow>
    </md>
    In other words,
    <me>
      J_t := v_{\rho\sigma}(\mathfrak{M}(t) \setminus \{0\}) \subseteq \mathbb{Z} \cap [mt, Mt].
    </me>
    For each <m>\beta \in J_t</m>, we take a <m>z_{\beta} \in \mathfrak{M}(t)</m> with <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(z_{\beta}) = \beta</m>. 
    We next prove that, for some <m>t \in \mathbb{N}</m> sufficiently large, there exists <m>H \in \mathfrak{M}(t)</m> such that
    <men xml:id="chvh3">
      [\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P), \ell_{\rho\sigma}(H)] \ne 0.
    </men>
    Assume by contradiction that
    <me>
      [\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P), \ell_{\rho\sigma}(H)] = 0 \qquad\text{for all } t \in \mathbb{N} \text{ and all } H \in \mathfrak{M}(t).
    </me>
    We claim that then
    <men xml:id="M_t_es_linspan">
      \mathfrak{M}(t) = \operatorname{linspan}\{z_{\beta} : \beta \in J_t\} \quad \text{for all } t \in \mathbb{N}.
    </men>
    In fact, suppose this equality is false for some <m>t</m>, and choose 
    <m>z \in \mathfrak{M}(t) \setminus \operatorname{linspan} \{z_{\beta} : \beta \in J_t\}</m> such that the number 
    <m>\gamma := v_{\rho\sigma}(z)</m> is minimum. By assumption,
    <me>
      [\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P), \ell_{\rho\sigma}(z)] = [\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P), \ell_{\rho\sigma}(z_{\gamma})] = 0.
    </me>
    Since <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(P) \ne 0</m>, by <xref ref="P_y_Q_alineados"/>(2b) and <xref ref="R_reducido"/>, there exist 
    <m>\lambda, \lambda_{\gamma} \in K^{\times}</m>, <m>n, n_{\gamma} \in \mathbb{N}_0</m> and a polynomial <m>R</m>, 
    which is not a proper power, such that
    <me>
      \ell_{\rho\sigma}(z) = \lambda R^n \quad\text{and}\quad \ell_{\rho\sigma}(z_{\gamma}) = \lambda_{\gamma} R^{n_{\gamma}}.
    </me>
    But, since <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(z) = v_{\rho\sigma}(z_{\gamma})</m> and <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(R) \ne 0</m>, by 
    <xref ref="pr_v_de_un_producto1"/>(4), we have <m>n = n_{\gamma}</m>. Hence,
    <me>
      v_{\rho\sigma}(z - \lambda \lambda_{\gamma}^{-1} z_{\gamma}) &lt; v_{\rho\sigma}(z)
      \quad\text{and}\quad
      z - \lambda \lambda_{\gamma}^{-1} z_{\gamma} \in \mathfrak{M}(t) \setminus \operatorname{linspan}\{z_{\beta} : \beta \in J_t\},
    </me>
    which contradicts the choice of <m>z</m> and thus proves <xref ref="M_t_es_linspan"/>, finishing the proof of the claim. 
    But <xref ref="M_t_es_linspan"/> implies <m>\dim \mathfrak{M}(t) \le |J_t| \le (M - m)t</m>, for all <m>t</m>, 
    which contradicts <xref ref="chvh2"/> if we take <m>t \ge M - m</m>. Thus, we can find <m>H \in K[P,Q]</m> such that 
    <xref ref="chvh3"/> is satisfied. We now define recursively <m>(H_j)_{j\ge 0}</m>, by setting
    <me>
      H_0 := H \quad\text{and}\quad H_{j+1} := [H_j, P].
    </me>
    Since <m>H_0 \in K[P,Q]</m> and <m>[P,Q] \in K^{\times}</m>, from <xref ref="P_Pm_Qn"/> it follows that eventually 
    <m>H_l = 0</m>. Let <m>k</m> be the largest index for which <m>H_k \ne 0</m>. By 
    <xref ref="re_v_de_un_conmutador"/>(3), we know that <m>[\ell_{\rho\sigma}(H_k), \ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)] = 0</m>. 
    But we also have <m>[\ell_{\rho\sigma}(H_0), \ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)] \ne 0</m> and so there exists a largest <m>j</m> 
    such that <m>[\ell_{\rho\sigma}(H_j), \ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)] \ne 0</m>. Hence, by 
    <xref ref="pr_v_de_un_conmutador"/>, we have 
    <m>[\ell_{\rho\sigma}(H_j), \ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)] = \ell_{\rho\sigma}(H_{j+1})</m>. Thus
    <me>
      [[\ell_{\rho\sigma}(H_j), \ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)], \ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)] = [\ell_{\rho\sigma}(H_{j+1}), \ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)] = 0,
    </me>
    and so <m>G_0 := H_j</m> satisfies the required conditions.
  </p>
</proof>

<proposition xml:id="prop_F0">
  <statement>
    <p>
      <title>Existence of F_0.</title>
      Let <m>(\rho,\sigma) \in \mathfrak{V}</m>, let <m>(P,Q)</m> be a Jacobian pair and let <m>R \in K[x,y]</m> be a 
      polynomial such that <m>\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P) \sim R^t</m> with <m>t &gt; 0</m> as large as possible. If 
      <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(P) \ne 0</m>, then there exists <m>k \ge 0</m> and a <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous element 
      <m>F_0 \in K[x,y]</m> such that <m>[R, F_0] \sim R^k</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</proposition>

<proof>
  <p>
    Let <m>G_0</m> be as in <xref ref="Lema_Joseph"/> and set <m>F_0 := \ell_{\rho\sigma}(G_0) \ne 0</m> and 
    <m>F_1 := [F_0, R]</m>. Since
    <me>
      [\ell_{\rho\sigma}(G_0), \ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)] \sim [F_0, R^t] = t R^{t-1} [F_0, R] \sim R^{t-1} F_1
    </me>
    and
    <me>
      [[\ell_{\rho\sigma}(G_0), \ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)], \ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)] \sim [R^{t-1} F_1, R^t]
      = t R^{2t-2} [F_1, R],
    </me>
    by <xref ref="Lema_Joseph"/>, we have <m>F_1 \ne 0</m> and <m>[F_1, R] = 0</m>. Thus, since 
    <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(R) = \frac{1}{t} v_{\rho\sigma}(P) \ne 0</m>, we can apply 
    <xref ref="P_y_Q_alineados"/>(2b), and we obtain that <m>F_1 \sim S^k</m> and <m>R \sim S^i</m>, for some 
    <m>i,k \ge 0</m> and some <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous element <m>S \in K[x,y]</m>. Note that 
    <m>i &gt; 0</m> because <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(R) \ne 0</m>. But, since <m>t</m> is maximal, we must have 
    <m>i = 1</m>, and so <m>S \sim R</m>. Hence <m>F_1 \sim R^k</m>, as desired.
  </p>
</proof>

<proposition xml:id="central_2">
  <statement>
    <p>
      <title>Centralizer computation.</title>
      Let <m>(\rho,\sigma) \in \mathfrak{V}_{&gt;0}</m> and let <m>R,F \in K[x,y]\setminus\{0\}</m> be 
      <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous elements. Assume that <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(R) \ge 0</m> and that there exists 
      <m>k \ge 1</m> such that
      <men xml:id="jacobiano_es_R__k">
        [R,F] = R^k.
      </men>
      Define <m>z := x^{-\sigma} y^{\rho} \in K[x,y,x^{-1},y^{-1}]</m>, and write
      <men xml:id="escritura_de_R">
        R = x^i y^j r(z) \quad\text{and}\quad F = x^u y^v f(z), \quad \text{with } r(z), f(z) \in K[z],\; f(0) \ne 0,\; r(0) \ne 0.
      </men>
      Then
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><m>v_{\rho\sigma}(F) = (k-1) v_{\rho\sigma}(R) + \rho + \sigma</m>.</li>
      <li>Every irreducible factor of <m>r(z)</m> divides <m>f(z)</m>.</li>
      <li>
        If <m>f(z)</m> is a constant (i.e., <m>F</m> is a monomial), then <m>r(z)</m> is also a constant 
        (i.e., <m>R</m> is a monomial).
      </li>
      <li>
        If <m>\deg(f(z)) = 1</m>, then <m>r(z) \sim (z - \lambda)^{\ell}</m>, for some 
        <m>\lambda \in K^{\times}</m> and <m>\ell \in \mathbb{N}_0</m>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <men xml:id="Desarrollo_de_R__k">
          R^k = x^{i+u-1} y^{j+v-1} \left( v_{\rho\sigma}(R) z r(z) f'(z) - v_{\rho\sigma}(F) z f(z) r'(z) + (iv - ju) r(z) f(z) \right).
        </men>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </statement>
</proposition>
 <proof>
  <p>
    Since <m>F</m> and <m>R</m> are <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous, by 
    <xref ref="re_v_de_un_conmutador"/>(1) and <xref ref="pr_v_de_un_producto1"/>(4) we have
    <me>
      v_{\rho\sigma}(R) + v_{\rho\sigma}(F) - (\rho+\sigma) = k v_{\rho\sigma}(R),
    </me>
    which yields item 1. By <xref ref="jacobiano_es_R__k"/>, <xref ref="escritura_de_R"/> and 
    <xref ref="propiedades_basicas_del_jacobiano"/>, we obtain that
    <men xml:id="R__k_primero">
      <md>
        <mrow>
          R^k &amp;= [x^i y^j r(z), x^u y^v f(z)]
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          &amp;= x^u y^v r(z)[x^i y^j, f(z)] + x^i y^j f(z)[r(z), x^u y^v] + r(z) f(z)[x^i y^j, x^u y^v]
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          &amp;= x^u y^v r(z) f'(z)[x^i y^j, z] + x^i y^j f(z) r'(z)[z, x^u y^v] + r(z) f(z)[x^i y^j, x^u y^v].
        </mrow>
      </md>
    </men>
    On the other hand, by <xref ref="calculo_de_st_y_en"/> we have
    <me>
      \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(R) = (i,j) \quad\text{and}\quad \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(F) = (u,v).
    </me>
    Hence
    <me>
      v_{\rho\sigma}(R) = v_{\rho\sigma}(\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(R)) = v_{\rho\sigma}(i,j) = \rho i + \sigma j
      \quad\text{and}\quad
      v_{\rho\sigma}(F) = \rho u + \sigma v,
    </me>
    and so, by <xref ref="determinante"/> we have
    <md>
      <mrow>
        [x^i y^j, x^u y^v] = (iv - ju) x^{i+u-1} y^{j+v-1},
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
        [x^i y^j, z] = [x^i y^j, x^{-\sigma} y^{\rho}] = (i\rho + j\sigma) x^{i-\sigma-1} y^{j+\rho-1}
        = v_{\rho\sigma}(R) z x^{i-1} y^{j-1},
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
        [z, x^u y^v] = [x^{-\sigma} y^{\rho}, x^u y^v] = -(\sigma v + \rho u) x^{u-\sigma-1} y^{v+\rho-1}
        = -v_{\rho\sigma}(F) z x^{u-1} y^{v-1}.
      </mrow>
    </md>
    Inserting these values into <xref ref="R__k_primero"/>, we obtain
    <me>
      R^k = x^{i+u-1} y^{j+v-1} \left( v_{\rho\sigma}(R) z r(z) f'(z) - v_{\rho\sigma}(F) z f(z) r'(z) + (iv - ju) r(z) f(z) \right),
    </me>
    which is item 5. Let <m>h(z)</m> be an irreducible factor of <m>r(z)</m> and let <m>m</m> and <m>n</m> be the multiplicity 
    of <m>h(z)</m> in <m>r(z)</m> and <m>f(z)</m>, respectively. Note that <m>h(z) \ne z</m>, since <m>r(0) \ne 0</m>. 
    We claim that <m>n &gt; 0</m>. In fact, since <m>k \ge 1</m>, by item 1 we have <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(F) \ne 0</m>. 
    Consequently, if <m>n = 0</m>, then computing the multiplicity of <m>h(z)</m> in each side of 
    <xref ref="Desarrollo_de_R__k"/>, we obtain that <m>k m = m - 1</m>, which is impossible. This proves item 2. 
    Items 3 and 4 follow directly from item 2, which concludes the proof.
  </p>
</proof>

<theorem xml:id="central">
  <statement>
    <p>
      <title>Centralizer theorem.</title>
      Let <m>(\rho,\sigma) \in \mathfrak{V}_{&gt;0}</m> and let <m>(P,Q)</m> be a Jacobian pair. If 
      <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(P) &gt; 0</m>, then there exists a <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous element 
      <m>F \in K[x,y]</m> such that
      <men xml:id="eq_central">
        [\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P), F] \sim \ell_{\rho\sigma}(P).
      </men>
      Moreover
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><m>v_{\rho\sigma}(F) = \rho + \sigma</m>,</li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P) \times \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(F) \ne 0</m> if and only if 
          <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(F) = (1,1)</m>,
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>\operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(P) \times \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(F) \ne 0</m> if and only if 
          <m>\operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(F) = (1,1)</m>,
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P) \times (1,1) \ne 0</m> and 
          <m>\operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(P) \times (1,1) \ne 0</m>,
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </statement>
</theorem>

<proof>
  <p>
    Let <m>R \in K[x,y]</m> be such that <m>\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P) \sim R^t</m> with <m>t &gt; 0</m> as large as possible. 
    Clearly <m>R</m> is <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous. Moreover, by <xref ref="prop_F0"/>, there exist a 
    <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous element <m>F_0 \in K[x,y]</m> and <m>k \ge 0</m> such that 
    <m>[R, F_0] \sim R^k</m>. We can and will assume that
    <men xml:id="ecuacion_para_F0">
      [R, F_0] = R^k.
    </men>
    Note that
    <men xml:id="desigualdad_para_v_R">
      v_{\rho\sigma}(R) = \frac{1}{t} v_{\rho\sigma}(P) &gt; 0
    </men>
    and that, by <xref ref="central_2"/>(1) and <xref ref="pr_v_de_un_producto1"/>(4),
    <me>
      v_{\rho\sigma}(F_0) = (k-1) v_{\rho\sigma}(R) + \rho + \sigma &gt; 0.
    </me>
    Hence <m>(0,0) \notin \{\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(F_0),\, \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(F_0),\, \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P),\, \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(P)\}</m>. 
    Next, we will prove that there exists a <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous element <m>F \in K[x,y]</m> such that
    <men xml:id="ecuacion_para_F">
      [R, F] = R.
    </men>
    If <m>k = 0</m>, we can take <m>F := F_0 R</m>. So, we can assume that <m>k \ge 1</m>. In this case, we will prove 
    that there exists a <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous element <m>F \in K[x,y]</m> such that
    <men xml:id="eq3">
      F_0 = F R^{k-1}.
    </men>
    This will finish the proof of <xref ref="ecuacion_para_F"/>, because it implies that
    <me>
      R^{k-1}[R, F] = [R, F R^{k-1}] = [R, F_0] = R^k,
    </me>
    and, since <m>K[x,y]</m> is a domain, we obtain <m>[R, F] = R</m>. Set <m>z := x^{-\sigma} y^{\rho}</m> and write
    <men xml:id="pepe1">
      R = x^i y^j r(z) \quad\text{and}\quad F_0 = x^u y^v f(z), \quad \text{with } r(z), f(z) \in K[z],\; f(0) \ne 0,\; r(0) \ne 0.
    </men>
    By <xref ref="central_2"/>(5), we have
    <men xml:id="LHS_2">
      R^k = x^{i+u-1} y^{j+v-1} \left( v_{\rho\sigma}(R) z r(z) f'(z) - v_{\rho\sigma}(F_0) z f(z) r'(z) + (iv - ju) r(z) f(z) \right).
    </men>
    Let <m>h(z)</m> be an irreducible factor of <m>r(z)</m> and let <m>m</m> and <m>n</m> be the multiplicity of 
    <m>h(z)</m> in <m>r(z)</m> and <m>f(z)</m>, respectively. By <xref ref="central_2"/>(2) we know that <m>n &gt; 0</m>. 
    Next we will prove that <m>n &gt; (k-1)m</m>. For this, we write <m>r(z) = h(z)^m \overline{r}(z)</m> and 
    <m>f(z) = h(z)^n \overline{f}(z)</m>. Then
    <me>
      r'(z) = h(z)^m \overline{r}'(z) + m h(z)^{m-1} h'(z) \overline{r}(z)
      \quad\text{and}\quad
      f'(z) = h(z)^n \overline{f}'(z) + n h(z)^{n-1} h'(z) \overline{f}(z),
    </me>
    and so <xref ref="LHS_2"/> yields
    <md>
      <mrow>
        R^k &amp;= x^{u+i-1} y^{v+j-1} \Biggl( v_{\rho\sigma}(R) z h(z)^m \overline{r}(z) 
        \left( h(z)^n \overline{f}'(z) + n h(z)^{n-1} h'(z) \overline{f}(z) \right)
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
        &amp;\qquad - v_{\rho\sigma}(F_0) z h(z)^n \overline{f}(z)
        \left( h(z)^m \overline{r}'(z) + m h(z)^{m-1} h'(z) \overline{r}(z) \right)
        + (iv - ju) h(z)^{m+n} \overline{r}(z) \overline{f}(z) \Biggr)
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
        &amp;= x^{u+i-1} y^{v+j-1} \Biggl( h(z)^{n+m-1} \overline{f}(z) \overline{r}(z) h'(z) z
        \left( v_{\rho\sigma}(R) n - v_{\rho\sigma}(F_0) m \right)
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
        &amp;\qquad + h(z)^{m+n} \Bigl( v_{\rho\sigma}(R) z \overline{r}(z) \overline{f}'(z)
        - v_{\rho\sigma}(F_0) z \overline{r}'(z) \overline{f}(z) + (iv - ju) \overline{r}(z) \overline{f}(z) \Bigr) \Biggr).
      </mrow>
    </md>
    The multiplicity of <m>h(z)</m> on the left hand side is <m>mk</m>. If 
    <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(R) n - v_{\rho\sigma}(F_0) m \ne 0</m>, then the multiplicity of <m>h(z)</m> on the 
    right hand side is <m>m + n - 1</m>, and so <m>mk = m + n - 1</m>, which implies 
    <m>n = m(k-1) + 1 &gt; m(k-1)</m>, as desired. If 
    <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(R) n - v_{\rho\sigma}(F_0) m = 0</m>, then, using item 1 of <xref ref="central_2"/> and 
    that <m>\rho+\sigma &gt; 0</m>, we obtain
    <me>
      v_{\rho\sigma}(R) n = v_{\rho\sigma}(F_0) m = m(k-1) v_{\rho\sigma}(R) + m(\rho+\sigma) &gt; m(k-1) v_{\rho\sigma}(R).
    </me>
    Since <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(R) &gt; 0</m>, this implies that <m>n &gt; m(k-1)</m>, also in this case. Since this is 
    true for each irreducible factor of <m>r(z)</m>, there exists <m>g(z) \in K[z]</m>, such that 
    <m>f(z) = r(z)^{k-1} g(z)</m>. But then
    <me>
      x^{i(k-1)} y^{j(k-1)} F_0 = x^{i(k-1)+u} y^{j(k-1)+v} f(z)
      = x^{i(k-1)+u} y^{j(k-1)+v} r(z)^{k-1} g(z) = x^u y^v g(z) R^{k-1},
    </me>
    and so
    <me>
      F := x^{u - i(k-1)} y^{v - j(k-1)} g(z) \in K[x,y,x^{-1},y^{-1}]
    </me>
    satisfies <xref ref="eq3"/>. We claim that <m>F \in K[x,y]</m>. Write 
    <m>g(z) = \sum_{\ell=0}^{\gamma} b_{\ell} z^{\ell}</m> with <m>b_{\gamma} \ne 0</m>. Since
    <me>
      F = x^{u - i(k-1)} y^{v - j(k-1)} \sum_{\ell=0}^{\gamma} b_{\ell} z^{\ell}
      = x^{u - i(k-1)} y^{v - j(k-1)} \sum_{\ell=0}^{\gamma} b_{\ell} x^{-\sigma \ell} y^{\rho \ell},
    </me>
    in order to prove the claim, we must show that
    <men xml:id="para_el_claim">
      u - i(k-1) - \sigma \ell \ge 0 \quad\text{and}\quad v - j(k-1) + \rho \ell \ge 0, \qquad \text{for } 0 \le \ell \le \gamma.
    </men>
    Clearly, for this will be sufficient to prove the cases <m>\ell = 0</m> and <m>\ell = \gamma</m>. We first show 
    that <m>u - i(k-1) \ge 0</m> and <m>v - j(k-1) \ge 0</m>. If 
    <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(R) \times \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(F_0) \ne 0</m>, then, by equality 
    <xref ref="ecuacion_para_F0"/>, <xref ref="extremos_no_alineados"/>(1) and <xref ref="st_y_en"/>, we have
    <me>
      \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(R) + \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(F_0) - (1,1) = k \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(R).
    </me>
    Since, by <xref ref="pepe1"/> and <xref ref="calculo_de_st_y_en"/> we know that 
    <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(F_0) = (u,v)</m> and <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(R) = (i,j)</m>, 
    this equation is equivalent to
    <me>
      \bigl( u - (k-1)i,\; v - (k-1)j \bigr) = (1,1).
    </me>
    Consequently,
    <me>
      u - i(k-1) = 1 \ge 0 \quad\text{and}\quad v - j(k-1) = 1 \ge 0,
    </me>
    as desired. On the other hand, if 
    <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(R) \times \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(F_0) = 0</m>, then
    <men xml:id="eq2">
      \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(F_0) = \lambda \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(R), \quad \text{for some } \lambda \in \mathbb{Q}^{\times},
    </men>
    and so,
    <me>
      \bigl( u - (k-1)i,\; v - (k-1)j \bigr) = \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(F_0) - (k-1) \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(R)
      = (\lambda - k + 1) \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(R).
    </me>
    Moreover, by <xref ref="eq2"/>, we have <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(F_0) = \lambda v_{\rho\sigma}(R)</m>, which combined with 
    <xref ref="desigualdad_para_v_R"/> and <xref ref="central_2"/>(1), gives
    <me>
      \lambda = \frac{v_{\rho\sigma}(F_0)}{v_{\rho\sigma}(R)} = k-1 + \frac{\rho+\sigma}{v_{\rho\sigma}(R)} &gt; k-1.
    </me>
    Hence <m>u - (k-1)i = (\lambda - (k-1)) i \ge 0</m> and 
    <m>v - (k-1)j = (\lambda - (k-1)) j \ge 0</m>, as desired. We now prove that
    <me>
      u - i(k-1) - \gamma \sigma \ge 0 \quad\text{and}\quad v - j(k-1) + \gamma \rho \ge 0.
    </me>
    Let <m>\alpha := \deg(f)</m> and <m>\beta := \deg(r)</m>. By <xref ref="pepe1"/>, 
    <xref ref="calculo_de_st_y_en"/> and the fact that <m>f(z) = r(z)^{k-1} g(z)</m>, we have
    <me>
      \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(F_0) = (u,v) + \alpha(-\sigma,\rho), \quad
      \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(R) = (i,j) + \beta(-\sigma,\rho) \quad\text{and}\quad
      \gamma = \alpha - (k-1)\beta.
    </me>
    Hence
    <men xml:id="pepe2">
      \bigl( u - (k-1)i - \gamma\sigma,\; v - (k-1)j + \gamma\rho \bigr)
      = \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(F_0) - (k-1) \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(R).
    </men>
    If <m>\operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(R) \times \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(F_0) \ne 0</m>, then, by equality 
    <xref ref="ecuacion_para_F0"/>, <xref ref="extremos_no_alineados"/>(2) and <xref ref="st_y_en"/>, we have
    <me>
      \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(R) + \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(F_0) - (1,1) = k \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(R)
      \iff \bigl( u - (k-1)i - \gamma\sigma,\; v - (k-1)j + \gamma\rho \bigr) = (1,1),
    </me>
    where, for the equivalence, we use <xref ref="pepe2"/>. Thus
    <me>
      u - i(k-1) - \gamma\sigma = 1 \ge 0 \quad\text{and}\quad v - j(k-1) + \gamma\rho = 1 \ge 0,
    </me>
    as desired. On the other hand, if 
    <m>\operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(R) \times \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(F_0) = 0</m>, then
    <men xml:id="eq2_prime">
      \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(F_0) = \lambda \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(R), \quad \text{for some } \lambda \in \mathbb{Q}^{\times},
    </men>
    and so, again by <xref ref="pepe2"/>,
    <me>
      \bigl( u - (k-1)i - \gamma\sigma,\; v - (k-1)j + \gamma\rho \bigr)
      = \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(F_0) - (k-1) \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(R)
      = (\lambda - k + 1) \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(R).
    </me>
    Moreover, by <xref ref="eq2_prime"/>, we have <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(F_0) = \lambda v_{\rho\sigma}(R)</m>, which combined with 
    <xref ref="desigualdad_para_v_R"/> and <xref ref="central_2"/>(1), gives
    <me>
      \lambda = \frac{v_{\rho\sigma}(F_0)}{v_{\rho\sigma}(R)} = k-1 + \frac{\rho+\sigma}{v_{\rho\sigma}(R)} &gt; k-1.
    </me>
    Since <m>(i - \beta\sigma,\; j + \beta\rho) = \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(R) \in \mathbb{N}_0 \times \mathbb{N}_0</m>, 
    this implies that
    <me>
      u - (k-1)i - \gamma\sigma = (\lambda - k + 1)(i - \beta\sigma) \ge 0
      \quad\text{and}\quad
      v - (k-1)j + \gamma\rho = (\lambda - k + 1)(j + \beta\rho) \ge 0,
    </me>
    as desired. This finishes the proof of <xref ref="para_el_claim"/>, and concludes the proof of 
    <xref ref="ecuacion_para_F"/>. But then
    <me>
      [\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P), F] \sim [R^t, F] = t R^{t-1}[R, F] = t R^t \sim \ell_{\rho\sigma}(P),
    </me>
    and so <xref ref="eq_central"/> is true. Finally we prove the additional statements. By 
    <xref ref="re_v_de_un_conmutador"/>(1),
    <me>
      [F, \ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)] \sim \ell_{\rho\sigma}(P) \Longrightarrow v_{\rho\sigma}(F) = \rho + \sigma,
    </me>
    which is statement 1. Statements 2 and 3 follow from <xref ref="extremos_no_alineados"/>. In order to prove 
    statement 4, we assume by contradiction that <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P) \times (1,1) = 0</m>. 
    By statement 2, necessarily
    <men xml:id="pepe3">
      \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(F) \ne (1,1),
    </men>
    and so, again by statement 2, we have <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P) \times \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(F) = 0</m>. 
    Since <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(F) \ne (0,0)</m> and <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P) \ne (0,0)</m> 
    (because <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(F) &gt; 0</m> and <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(P) &gt; 0</m>),
    <me>
      \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P) \times \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(F) = 0
      \quad\text{and}\quad
      \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P) \times (1,1) = 0
      \Longrightarrow \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(F) \sim \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P) \sim (1,1).
    </me>
    Hence, there exists <m>\lambda \in \mathbb{Q}</m> such that <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(F) = \lambda (1,1)</m>. 
    But then <m>\lambda = 1</m>, because
    <me>
      \lambda (\rho + \sigma) = v_{\rho\sigma}(\lambda (1,1)) = v_{\rho\sigma}(\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(F)) = v_{\rho\sigma}(F) = \rho + \sigma.
    </me>
    So <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(F) = (1,1)</m>, which contradicts <xref ref="pepe3"/> and proves that 
    <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P) \times (1,1) \ne 0</m>. By a similar argument we obtain 
    <m>\operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(P) \times (1,1) \ne 0</m>, which concludes the proof of item 4 and of the theorem.
  </p>
</proof>
<remark xml:id="sobre_central">
  <p>
    Assume that <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>, <m>P</m> and <m>F</m> are as in <xref ref="central"/>. Since
    <me>
      v_{\rho\sigma}(P) &gt; 0 \quad\text{and}\quad v_{\rho\sigma}(F) = \rho + \sigma &gt; 0,
    </me>
    we know that <m>(0,0) \notin \{\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(F),\, \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(F), \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P),\, \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(P)\}</m>. 
    Hence, items 2 and 3 of <xref ref="central"/> are equivalent to
    <md>
      <mrow>
        \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P) \sim \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(F) \text{ if and only if } \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(F) \ne (1,1)
      </mrow>
      <intertext>and</intertext>
      <mrow>
        \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(P) \sim \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(F) \text{ if and only if } \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(F) \ne (1,1),
      </mrow>
    </md>
    respectively.
  </p>
</remark>

<subsection xml:id="ejercicios_capitulo5">
  <title>Exercises</title>

  <exercise xml:id="ejercicio_5_1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\frac{P}{Q} \in K(x,y)</m> be a rational function. Use that <m>K(x,y)</m> is naturally included in 
        the field <m>K((x,y,x^{-1},y^{-1}))</m> of Laurent series, in order to prove that if 
        <m>\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(\frac{P}{Q}\right) = \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(\frac{P}{Q}\right) = 0</m>, 
        then <m>\frac{P}{Q} \in K</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ejercicio_5_5">
    <statement>
      <p>Prove equality <xref ref="ejercicio_mdc"/>.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <!-- 
    Comentario: Solución del Ejercicio 5.5
    We have
    m(a,b) = mc(r,s) + md(u,v) = (mcr + dmu, mcs + mdv) = (mcr + dnr, mcs + dns) = (r,s)
    and
    n(a,b) = nc(r,s) + nd(u,v) = (ncr + dnu, ncs + ndv) = (mcu + dnu, mcv + dnv) = (u,v)
  -->

  <exercise xml:id="ejercicio_5_2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>R = x + 2x^2 y^3 + x^3 y^6</m> and <m>F_0 = x^2 y + 3x^3 y^4 + 3x^4 y^7 + x^5 y^{10}</m>. 
        Verify that <m>[R, F_0] = R^2</m> and find <m>F</m> with <m>[R, F] = R</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <figure xml:id="fig_13">
    <caption>Hint for <xref ref="ejercicio_5_2"/></caption>
    <image width="60%" source="img/img13.png"/>
  </figure>

</subsection>

    
  </section>
  <!-- ===================== -->
  <!-- SECTION 1.6 -->
  <!-- ===================== -->

  <section xml:id="sec-1-6">
    <title>Order on directions and shaping the support</title>
    
   <definition xml:id="intervalos_en_direcciones">
  <statement>
    <p>
      We assign to each direction <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m> its corresponding unit vector in <m>S^1</m> (that is 
      <m>\frac{1}{\|(\rho,\sigma)\|}(\rho,\sigma)</m>), and we define an <term>interval</term> in <m>\mathfrak{V}</m> 
      <idx>interval in <m>\mathfrak{V}</m></idx> as the preimage under this map of an arc of <m>S^1</m> that is not 
      the whole circle. We consider each interval endowed with the order that increases counterclockwise. 
      For example, given <m>(\rho_1,\sigma_1),(\rho_2,\sigma_2)\in \mathfrak{V}</m>, we let 
      <m>[(\rho_1,\sigma_1),(\rho_2,\sigma_2)]</m> denote the preimage of the arc from 
      <m>\frac{1}{\|(\rho_1,\sigma_1)\|}(\rho_1,\sigma_1)</m> to 
      <m>\frac{1}{\|(\rho_2,\sigma_2)\|}(\rho_2,\sigma_2)</m> in the counterclockwise direction, that contains both 
      endpoints. This interval is the set
      <me>
        [(\rho_1,\sigma_1),(\rho_2,\sigma_2)] = \{c \in \mathfrak{V} : (\rho_1,\sigma_1) \le c \le (\rho_2,\sigma_2) \},
      </me>
      where the order is the one mentioned above. In a similar way we can define the open and semiopen intervals 
      <m>](\rho_1,\sigma_1),(\rho_2,\sigma_2)[</m>, <m>](\rho_1,\sigma_1),(\rho_2,\sigma_2)]</m> and 
      <m>[(\rho_1,\sigma_1),(\rho_2,\sigma_2)[</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>

<definition xml:id="forma_debil">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>P \in K[x,y]\setminus\{0\}</m>. We define the set of <term>directions of <m>P</m></term> 
      <idx>directions of a polynomial</idx>, as
      <me>
        \operatorname{Dir}(P) := \{(\rho,\sigma) \in \mathfrak{V} : \#\operatorname{Supp}(\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)) &gt; 1\}.
      </me>
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>

<figure xml:id="fig_14">
  <caption>An example of <m>\operatorname{Dir}(P)</m></caption>
  <image width="100%" source="img/img14.png"/>
</figure>

<definition xml:id="Sucesor_y_predecesor">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>P \in K[x,y]\setminus\{0\}</m> which is not a monomial and <m>(\rho,\sigma) \in \mathfrak{V}</m>. 
      We define the <term>successor</term> <idx>successor</idx> <m>\operatorname{Succ}_P(\rho,\sigma)</m> of 
      <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m> to be the first element of <m>\operatorname{Dir}(P)</m> that one encounters starting from 
      <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m> and running counterclockwise, and the <term>predecessor</term> <idx>predecessor</idx> 
      <m>\operatorname{Pred}_P(\rho,\sigma)</m>, to be the first one, if we run clockwise.
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>

<remark xml:id="a_180_grados">
  <p>
    Note that if <m>P \in K[x,y]\setminus\{0\}</m> is a monomial, then <m>\operatorname{Dir}(P) = \emptyset</m> and 
    that if <m>P \in K[x,y]\setminus\{0\}</m> is <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous, but is not a monomial, then 
    <m>\operatorname{Dir}(P) = \{(\rho,\sigma), (-\rho,-\sigma)\}</m>. In this case
    <me>
      \operatorname{Pred}_P(\rho,\sigma) = \operatorname{Succ}_P(\rho,\sigma) = (-\rho,-\sigma).
    </me>
    Furthermore, if <m>P \in K[x,y]\setminus\{0\}</m> is not homogeneous, then any two consecutive directions of 
    <m>P</m> are separated by less than <m>180^{\circ}</m>, and so 
    <m>\operatorname{Pred}_P(\rho,\sigma) \ne \operatorname{Succ}_P(\rho,\sigma)</m>.
  </p>
</remark>

<remark xml:id="direcciones_consecutivas">
  <p>
    Let <m>P \in K[x,y]\setminus\{0\}</m> and let <m>(\rho_1,\sigma_1)</m> and <m>(\rho_2,\sigma_2)</m> be two 
    consecutive directions of <m>P</m>. For any direction <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m> with 
    <m>(\rho_1,\sigma_1) &lt; (\rho,\sigma) &lt; (\rho_2,\sigma_2)</m>, we have
    <me>
      \operatorname{en}_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(P) = \operatorname{Supp}(\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)) = \operatorname{stn}_{\rho_2,\sigma_2}(P).
    </me>
  </p>
</remark>

<remark xml:id="direcciones_consecutivas_prime">
  <p>
    If <m>I</m> is an interval in <m>\mathfrak{V}</m>, smaller than a closed half circle, which means there is no 
    <m>(\rho,\sigma) \in I</m> with <m>(-\rho,-\sigma) \in I</m>, then, for <m>(\rho,\sigma), (\rho',\sigma') \in I</m>, we have
    <men xml:id="order">
      (\rho,\sigma) &lt; (\rho',\sigma') \Longleftrightarrow (\rho,\sigma) \times (\rho',\sigma') &gt; 0.
    </men>
  </p>
</remark>

<proposition xml:id="pr_ell_por_automorfismos">
  <statement>
    <p>
      For <m>k \in \mathbb{N}_0</m> and <m>\lambda \in K^{\times}</m>, consider the automorphism <m>\varphi</m> of 
      <m>K[x,y]</m> defined by <m>\varphi(x) := x</m> and <m>\varphi(y) := y + \lambda x^k</m>, and set 
      <m>(\rho,\sigma) := (1,k)</m>. For all <m>P \in K[x,y]\setminus\{0\}</m> and each direction 
      <m>(\rho_1,\sigma_1)</m> such that <m>(\rho,\sigma) &lt; (\rho_1,\sigma_1) &lt; (-\rho,-\sigma)</m>, we have
      <men xml:id="equa3">
        \ell_{\rho\sigma}(\varphi(P)) = \varphi(\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)), \quad
        \ell_{-\rho,-\sigma}(\varphi(P)) = \varphi(\ell_{-\rho,-\sigma}(P)) \quad\text{and}\quad
        \ell_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(\varphi(P)) = \ell_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(P).
      </men>
      Moreover <m>\operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(\varphi(P)) = \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m>, and so 
      <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(\varphi(P)) = v_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</proposition>

<figure xml:id="fig_15">
  <caption>Illustration of <xref ref="pr_ell_por_automorfismos"/></caption>
  <image width="100%" source="img/img15.png"/>
</figure>

<proof>
  <p>
    Since <m>\varphi</m> is <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous (see <xref ref="ejemplo_de_auto_homo"/>), for all 
    <m>P \in K[x,y]\setminus\{0\}</m>, we have
    <me>
      \ell_{\rho\sigma}(\varphi(P)) = \varphi(\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)) \qquad\text{and}\qquad 
      \ell_{-\rho,-\sigma}(\varphi(P)) = \varphi(\ell_{-\rho,-\sigma}(P)).
    </me>
    We next prove that <m>\ell_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(\varphi(P)) = \ell_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(P)</m>. To begin note that, by 
    <xref ref="direcciones_consecutivas_prime"/>,
    <me>
      \sigma_1 - k\rho_1 = (1,k) \times (\rho_1,\sigma_1) = (\rho,\sigma) \times (\rho_1,\sigma_1) &gt; 0,
    </me>
    and so <m>v_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(y) = \sigma_1 &gt; k\rho_1 = v_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(x^k)</m>. Consequently, 
    <m>\ell_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}\bigl(y + \lambda x^k\bigr) = y</m>, which implies that
    <men xml:id="pepe4">
      \ell_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}\bigl(\varphi(x^i y^j)\bigr) = \ell_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}\bigl(x^i (y + \lambda x^k)^j\bigr)
      = \ell_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(x)^i \ell_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(y + \lambda x^k)^j = x^i y^j,
    </men>
    where we use <xref ref="pr_v_de_un_producto1"/>(1). We claim that
    <men xml:id="equa2">
      \ell_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(\varphi(Q)) = Q \quad \text{for all } (\rho_1,\sigma_1)\text{-homogeneous } Q \in K[x,y]\setminus\{0\}.
    </men>
    In fact, let <m>Q</m> be a <m>(\rho_1,\sigma_1)</m>-homogeneous polynomial. Write <m>Q = \sum a_{ij} x^i y^j</m>, 
    where the sum runs on the support of <m>Q</m>. By <xref ref="pepe4"/>,
    <me>
      v_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(\varphi(a_{ij}x^i y^j)) = v_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(\ell_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(\varphi(a_{ij}x^i y^j)))
      = v_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(a_{ij}x^i y^j) = v_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(Q) \quad \text{for all } a_{ij}.
    </me>
    Since, moreover, <m>\sum \ell_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(\varphi(a_{ij}x^i y^j)) = \sum a_{ij} x^i y^j = Q \ne 0</m>, by 
    <xref ref="pr_v_de_un_producto1"/>(2), we obtain <xref ref="equa2"/>. Now write
    <me>
      P = P_1 + P_2 + \cdots + P_r, \quad \text{where each } P_i \text{ is } (\rho_1,\sigma_1)\text{-homogeneous and }
      v_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(P_1) &gt; v_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(P_2) &gt; \cdots.
    </me>
    Since, by <xref ref="equa2"/>
    <me>
      \varphi(P) = \varphi(P_1) + \varphi(P_2) + \cdots + \varphi(P_r)
      \quad\text{and}\quad
      v_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(\varphi(P_i)) = v_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(\ell_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(\varphi(P_i))) = v_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(P_i),
    </me>
    using again <xref ref="equa2"/> and <xref ref="pr_v_de_un_producto1"/>(3), we obtain
    <me>
      \ell_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(\varphi(P)) = \ell_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(\varphi(P_1)) = P_1 = \ell_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(P),
    </me>
    as desired. Now, since <m>(\rho,\sigma) &lt; (-\sigma,\rho) &lt; (-\rho,-\sigma)</m>, it follows from the first and last 
    equality in <xref ref="equa3"/>, that
    <me>
      \ell_{-\sigma,\rho}(\ell_{\rho\sigma}(\varphi(P))) = \ell_{-\sigma,\rho}(\varphi(\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)))
      = \ell_{-\sigma,\rho}(\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)).
    </me>
    Thus, by the second equality in <xref ref="eq_st_y_en"/>, we have 
    <m>\operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(\varphi(P)) = \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m>, which concludes the proof.
  </p>
</proof>

<corollary xml:id="de_pr_ell_por_automorfismos">
  <statement>
    <p>
      For <m>k \in \mathbb{N}_0</m> and <m>\lambda \in K^{\times}</m>, consider the automorphism 
      <m>\phi \colon K[x,y] \to K[x,y]</m>, defined by <m>\phi(x) := x + \lambda y^k</m> and <m>\phi(y) := y</m>, 
      and set <m>(\rho,\sigma) := (k,1)</m>. For all <m>P \in K[x,y]\setminus\{0\}</m> and each direction 
      <m>(\rho_1,\sigma_1)</m> such that <m>(-\rho,-\sigma) &lt; (\rho_1,\sigma_1) &lt; (\rho,\sigma)</m>, we have
      <men xml:id="ecqua3">
        \ell_{\rho\sigma}(\phi(P)) = \phi(\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)), \quad
        \ell_{-\rho,-\sigma}(\phi(P)) = \phi(\ell_{-\rho,-\sigma}(P)) \quad\text{and}\quad
        \ell_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(\phi(P)) = \ell_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(P).
      </men>
      Moreover <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(\phi(P)) = \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m>, and so 
      <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(\phi(P)) = v_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</corollary>

<proof>
  <p>
    Let <m>\varphi := \tau \circ \phi \circ \tau</m>, where <m>\tau</m> is as in <xref ref="acerca_del_flip"/>. 
    It is clear that <m>\varphi(x) = x</m> and <m>\varphi(y) = y + \lambda x^k</m>. Hence we can apply the previous 
    proposition in order to obtain
    <md>
      <mrow>
        \ell_{\sigma\rho}(\varphi(P)) = \varphi(\ell_{\sigma\rho}(P)), \qquad
        \ell_{-\sigma,-\rho}(\varphi(P)) = \varphi(\ell_{-\sigma,-\rho}(P)),
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
        \ell_{\sigma_1,\rho_1}(\varphi(P)) = \ell_{\sigma_1,\rho_1}(P), \qquad
        \operatorname{en}_{\sigma\rho}(\varphi(P)) = \operatorname{en}_{\sigma\rho}(P),
      </mrow>
    </md>
    for all <m>P \in K[x,y]</m>. Using <xref ref="acerca_del_flip"/>, we obtain <xref ref="ecqua3"/> and that 
    <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(\phi(P)) = \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m>. For example
    <me>
      \ell_{\rho\sigma}(\phi(P)) = \ell_{\rho\sigma}(\tau \circ \varphi \circ \tau(P))
      = \tau(\ell_{\sigma\rho}(\varphi \circ \tau(P)))
      = \tau \circ \varphi(\ell_{\sigma\rho}(\tau(P)))
      = \tau \circ \varphi \circ \tau (\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)) = \phi(\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)).
    </me>
    We leave the remaining equalities as an exercise for the reader.
  </p>
</proof>

<remark xml:id="convencion_automorfismos">
  <p>
    Let <m>\varphi</m> be an automorphism of <m>K[x,y]</m>. To streamline the exposition from this point onward, 
    we will occasionally refer to the expression <m>\varphi(P)</m> more informally. Instead of writing it explicitly 
    each time, we may say that we <q>apply <m>\varphi</m> to <m>P</m></q> or we <q>transform <m>P</m> by <m>\varphi</m></q>. 
    Furthermore, when the context is clear and no ambiguity arises, we may continue to denote the transformed 
    polynomial simply as <m>P</m>. This convention will be adopted throughout the remainder of the text.
  </p>
</remark>



  <remark xml:id="casos_shaping">
  <p>
    When we apply <m>\varphi</m> of <xref ref="pr_ell_por_automorfismos"/>, we will sometimes say that 
    <m>\varphi</m> <q>cuts</q>, <q>extends</q> or <q>erases</q> the edge 
    <m>H(\operatorname{Supp}(\ell_{1k}(P)))</m>, or <q>creates</q> the edge 
    <m>H(\operatorname{Supp}(\ell_{1k}(\varphi(P))))</m>. This has a precise meaning that we will explain. Write
  </p>

<p>
  <me>
    \ell_{1k}(P) \sim x^j g(z) \quad \text{with } z := x^{-k}y \text{ and } g(z) \in K[z] \text{ a monic polynomial.}
  </me>
  Assume that <m>n := \deg(g(z)) &gt; 0</m>. Since <m>K</m> is algebraically closed,
  <me>
    g(z) = z^{m_0} \prod_{i=1}^r (z - \lambda_i)^{m_i}
    \quad \text{with } \lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_r \in K^{\times} \text{ all different, }
    m_0 \in \mathbb{N}_0,\; m_1,\dots,m_r \in \mathbb{N} \text{ and } \sum_{i=0}^r m_i = n.
  </me>
  Note that, by the first equality in <xref ref="eq_st_y_en"/> and the fact that <m>v_{k,-1}(z) = -k^2 - 1 &lt; 0</m>,
  <men xml:id="equa4">
    \operatorname{stn}_{1k}(\ell_{1k}(P)) = (j - k m_0, m_0)
  </men>
  and
  <men xml:id="equa4_prime">
    \operatorname{en}_{1k}(\ell_{1k}(P)) = (j - k n, n).
  </men>
  Note also that <m>\varphi(x) = x</m> and <m>\varphi(y) = y + \lambda x^k</m> implies <m>\varphi(z) = z + \lambda</m> 
  (we can extend in a unique way <m>\varphi</m> to an automorphism of <m>K[x,x^{-1},y]</m>, which contains <m>z</m>), and so
  <men xml:id="equa4_prime_prime">
    \ell_{1k}(\varphi(P)) = \varphi(\ell_{1k}(P)) \sim x^j (z + \lambda)^{m_0} \prod_{i=1}^r (z + \lambda - \lambda_i)^{m_i}.
  </men>
  We consider the following cases:
</p>

  <p>
  <m>\textbf{a)}</m> 
  <m>(m_0,r) \ne (0,1)</m> and <m>\lambda = \lambda_i</m>, for some <m>i</m> with <m>m_i &gt; m_0</m>.
  By equality <xref ref="equa4"/> applied to <m>\ell_{1k}(\varphi(P))</m>, we have
  <me>
    \operatorname{stn}_{1k}(\varphi(P)) = (j - k m_i, m_i).
  </me>
  Since <m>v_{01}(\operatorname{stn}_{1k}(\varphi(P))) &gt; v_{01}(\operatorname{stn}_{1k}(P))</m>, we say that 
  <m>\varphi</m> <em>cuts</em> the edge <m>H(\operatorname{Supp}(\ell_{1k}(P)))</m>.
  <idx><m>\varphi</m> cuts the edge <m>H(\operatorname{Supp}(\ell_{1k}(P)))</m></idx>
</p>

  <p>
    In <xref ref="caso_a"/>, we illustrate this case with
    <me>
      k = 2 \quad\text{and}\quad P = 1 + x^8 + x^{10} z (z-1)^2 (z-2), \quad \text{where } z = x^{-2}y.
    </me>
    Thus
    <me>
      \ell_{12}(P) = x^{10} z (z-1)^2 (z-2), \quad m_0 = 1 \quad\text{and}\quad m_1 = 2.
    </me>
    We take <m>\lambda = \lambda_1 = 1</m> (so <m>\varphi(x) = x</m> and <m>\varphi(y) = y + x^2</m>). A direct computation, 
    using that <m>\varphi(z) = z + 1</m>, gives
    <me>
      \varphi(P) = 1 + x^8 + x^{10} z^2 (z+1)(z-1),
    </me>
    and so <m>\ell_{12}(\varphi(P)) = x^{10} z^2 (z+1)(z-1)</m> and 
    <m>\operatorname{stn}_{12}(\varphi(P)) = (6,2)</m>.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="caso_a">
    <caption>Case a</caption>
    <image width="90%" source="img/img16.png"/>
  </figure>

  <p>
    <bold>b)</bold> 
    <em><m>m_0 &gt; 0</m>, <m>r &gt; 0</m> and <m>\lambda \notin \{\lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_r\}</m> or 
    <m>\lambda = \lambda_i</m> for some <m>i</m> with <m>m_i &lt; m_0</m>.</em> 
    By equality <xref ref="equa4"/> applied to <m>\ell_{1k}(\varphi(P))</m>, we have
    <me>
      \operatorname{stn}_{1k}(\varphi(P)) = 
      \begin{cases} 
        (j - k m_i, m_i) &amp; \text{if } \lambda = \lambda_i \text{ for some } i, \\
        (j, 0) &amp; \text{otherwise.}
      \end{cases}
    </me>
    Since <m>v_{01}(\operatorname{stn}_{1k}(\varphi(P))) &lt; v_{01}(\operatorname{stn}_{1k}(P))</m>, we say that 
    <em><m>\varphi</m> extends</em> the edge <m>H(\operatorname{Supp}(\ell_{1k}(P)))</m>.
    <idx><m>\varphi</m> extends the edge <m>H(\operatorname{Supp}(\ell_{1k}(P)))</m></idx>
  </p>

  <p>
    In <xref ref="caso_b"/>, we illustrate this case with
    <me>
      k = 2 \quad\text{and}\quad P = 1 + x^8 + x^{10} z (z-1)^2 (z-2), \quad \text{where } z = x^{-2}y.
    </me>
    Thus
    <me>
      \ell_{12}(P) = x^{10} z (z-1)^2 (z-2) \quad\text{and}\quad m_0 = 1.
    </me>
    We take <m>\lambda = -1</m> (so <m>\varphi(x) = x</m> and <m>\varphi(y) = y - x^2</m>). A direct computation, 
    using that <m>\varphi(z) = z - 1</m>, gives
    <me>
      \varphi(P) = 1 + x^8 + x^{10} (z-1)(z-2)^2 (z-3),
    </me>
    and so <m>\ell_{12}(\varphi(P)) = x^{10} (z-1)(z-2)^2 (z-3)</m> and 
    <m>\operatorname{stn}_{12}(\varphi(P)) = (10,0)</m>.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="caso_b">
    <caption>Case b</caption>
    <image width="90%" source="img/img17.png"/>
  </figure>

  <p>
    <bold>c)</bold> 
    <em><m>m_0 = 0</m>, <m>r = 1</m> and <m>\lambda = \lambda_1</m>.</em> 
    By equalities <xref ref="equa4"/> and <xref ref="equa4_prime"/> applied to <m>\ell_{1k}(\varphi(P))</m>, we have
    <me>
      \operatorname{stn}_{1k}(\varphi(P)) = \operatorname{en}_{1k}(\varphi(P)) = (j - k n, n).
    </me>
    Hence <em><m>\varphi</m> erases</em> the edge <m>H(\operatorname{Supp}(\ell_{1k}(P)))</m>.
    <idx><m>\varphi</m> erases the edge <m>H(\operatorname{Supp}(\ell_{1k}(P)))</m></idx>
  </p>

  <p>
    In <xref ref="caso_c"/>, we illustrate this case with
    <me>
      k = 2 \quad\text{and}\quad P = 1 + x^6 + x^8 (z-1)^3, \quad \text{where } z = x^{-2}y.
    </me>
    Thus <m>\ell_{12}(P) = x^8 (z-1)^3</m>. We take <m>\lambda = \lambda_1 = 1</m> (so <m>\varphi(x) = x</m> and 
    <m>\varphi(y) = y + x^2</m>). A direct computation, using that <m>\varphi(z) = z + 1</m>, gives
    <me>
      \varphi(P) = 1 + x^6 + x^8 z^3,
    </me>
    and so <m>\ell_{12}(\varphi(P)) = x^8 z^3</m> and 
    <m>\operatorname{stn}_{12}(\varphi(P)) = \operatorname{en}_{12}(\varphi(P)) = (2,3)</m>.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="caso_c">
    <caption>Case c</caption>
    <image width="90%" source="img/img18.png"/>
  </figure>

  <p>
    <bold>d)</bold> 
    <em><m>m_0 &gt; 0</m>, <m>r = 0</m> and <m>\lambda \in K^{\times}</m>.</em> 
    By equalities <xref ref="equa4"/> and <xref ref="equa4_prime"/> applied to <m>\ell_{1k}(\varphi(P))</m>, we have
    <me>
      \operatorname{stn}_{1k}(\varphi(P)) = (j, 0) \ne (j - k m_0, m_0) = \operatorname{en}_{1k}(\varphi(P)).
    </me>
    Since <m>\ell_{1k}(P)</m> is a monomial, we say that <em><m>\varphi</m> creates</em> the edge 
    <m>H(\operatorname{Supp}(\ell_{1k}(\varphi(P))))</m>.
    <idx><m>\varphi</m> creates the edge <m>H(\operatorname{Supp}(\ell_{1k}(\varphi(P))))</m></idx>
  </p>

  <p>
    In <xref ref="caso_d"/>, we illustrate this case with
    <me>
      k = 1 \quad\text{and}\quad P = x^2 y + x^3 y + x^2 y^2 + x^3 y^2.
    </me>
    Thus <m>\ell_{11}(P) = x^3 y^2 = x^5 z^2</m>, where <m>z := x^{-1} y</m>. We take <m>\lambda = 1</m> 
    (so <m>\varphi(x) = x</m> and <m>\varphi(y) = y + x</m>). A direct computation, using that 
    <m>\varphi(z) = z + 1</m>, gives
    <me>
      \varphi(P) = x^3 (z+1) + x^4 (z+1)(z+2) + x^5 (z+1)^2,
    </me>
    and so <m>\ell_{11}(\varphi(P)) = x^5 (z+1)^2</m> and <m>\operatorname{stn}_{12}(\varphi(P)) = (5,0)</m>.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="caso_d">
    <caption>Case d</caption>
    <image width="90%" source="img/img19.png"/>
  </figure>

</remark>
    
  </section>
  <section xml:id="sec-1-7">
    <title>The shape of counterexamples and (m,n)-pairs</title>
    
    <p>
  We will see that a (hypothetical) counterexample to the plane Jacobian conjecture can be transformed into a 
  standard counterexample (see <xref ref="standard_counterexample"/>).
</p>

<remark xml:id="remark_monomial_counterexample">
  <p>
    By equality <xref ref="determinante"/>, one can readily verify that if <m>(P,Q)</m> is a Jacobian pair and 
    <m>P</m> is a monomial, then necessarily <m>P \sim x</m> or <m>P \sim y</m>. Consequently, by 
    <xref ref="casos_pequenhos"/> and version 2 of the Plane Jacobian conjecture, if <m>(P,Q)</m> is a counterexample 
    to the Jacobian conjecture, then neither <m>P</m> nor <m>Q</m> can be a monomial.
  </p>
</remark>

<p>
  From now on the previous remark will be used without explicit mention.
</p>

<definition xml:id="Smp">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>m,n \in \mathbb{N}</m> be coprime with <m>n,m &gt; 1</m>. A pair <m>(P,Q)</m>, of elements in <m>K[x,y]</m>, 
      is called an <term><m>(m,n)</m>-pair in <m>K[x,y]</m></term> <idx><m>(m,n)</m>-pair in <m>K[x,y]</m></idx>, if
      <me>
        [P,Q] \in K^{\times}, \quad
        \frac{v_{11}(P)}{v_{11}(Q)} = \frac{v_{10}(P)}{v_{10}(Q)} = \frac{m}{n}
        \quad\text{and}\quad
        v_{1,-1}(\operatorname{en}_{10}(P)) &lt; 0.
      </me>
      An <m>(m,n)</m>-pair <m>(P,Q)</m> is called <term>a standard <m>(m,n)</m>-pair in <m>K[x,y]</m></term> 
      <idx>standard <m>(m,n)</m>-pair in <m>K[x,y]</m></idx> if <m>v_{1,-1}(\operatorname{stn}_{10}(P)) &lt; 0</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>

<remark xml:id="imposible_que_P_y_Q_esten_encima_de_la_diagonal">
  <p>
    If <m>(P,Q)</m> is a Jacobian pair, then <m>v_{1,-1}(P) &gt; 0</m> or <m>v_{1,-1}(Q) &gt; 0</m>. In fact, by 
    <xref ref="pr_v_de_un_conmutador"/>, we have
    <men xml:id="desigualdad_para_commutador_1_1">
      v_{1,-1}(P) + v_{1,-1}(Q) - (1-1) \ge v_{1,-1}([P,Q]) = 0.
    </men>
    Assume by contradiction that <m>v_{1,-1}(P) \le 0</m> and <m>v_{1,-1}(Q) \le 0</m>. Then 
    <m>v_{1,-1}(P) = v_{1,-1}(Q) = 0</m> and <xref ref="desigualdad_para_commutador_1_1"/> becomes an equality. 
    Hence, on one hand, by <xref ref="pr_v_de_un_conmutador"/>, we have 
    <m>[\ell_{1,-1}(P), \ell_{1,-1}(Q)] \ne 0</m>; while, on the other hand, from 
    <m>v_{1,-1}(P) = v_{1,-1}(Q) = 0</m>, it follows that <m>\ell_{1,-1}(P), \ell_{1,-1}(Q) \in K[z]</m>, for <m>z = xy</m>, 
    which leads to the contradiction <m>[\ell_{1,-1}(P), \ell_{1,-1}(Q)] = 0</m>.
  </p>
</remark>

<remark xml:id="ve_rho_sigma_es_mayor_que_el_de_F">
  <p>
    Note that if <m>\rho \ge -\sigma &gt; 0</m>, and <m>i,j \in \mathbb{N}_0</m> with <m>i &gt; j</m>, then
    <me>
      v_{\rho\sigma}(i,j) = \rho i + \sigma j &gt; \rho i + \sigma i = (\rho + \sigma) i \ge \rho + \sigma \ge 0.
    </me>
  </p>
</remark>

<proposition xml:id="no_se_dividen">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Assume the Jacobian conjecture is false and let <m>(P,Q)</m> be a counterexample to it. If 
      <m>v_{11}(P) \mid v_{11}(Q)</m> or <m>v_{11}(Q) \mid v_{11}(P)</m>, then there is a counterexample 
      <m>(\tilde{P}, \tilde{Q})</m> with
      <me>
        \gcd(v_{11}(\tilde{P}), v_{11}(\tilde{Q})) &lt; \gcd(v_{11}(P), v_{11}(Q)).
      </me>
    </p>
  </statement>
</proposition>

<proof>
  <p>
    Assume for example that <m>v_{11}(P) \mid v_{11}(Q)</m> and write <m>v_{11}(Q) = k v_{11}(P)</m>. By 
    <xref ref="casos_pequenhos"/>, we know that <m>v_{11}(P) + v_{11}(Q) \ge 3</m>. Hence
    <me>
      v_{11}(P) + v_{11}(Q) - v_{11}(1,1) \ge 3 - 2 &gt; 0 = v_{11}([P,Q]).
    </me>
    Then, by <xref ref="pr_v_de_un_conmutador"/>, we have <m>[\ell_{11}(P), \ell_{11}(Q)] = 0</m>. Since, moreover 
    <m>v_{11}(P) &gt; 0</m>, by <xref ref="P_y_Q_alineados"/>(2b), there exist <m>m,n \in \mathbb{N}</m> and a 
    <m>(1,1)</m>-homogeneous polynomial <m>R</m>, such that
    <me>
      \ell_{11}(P) \sim R^m \qquad\text{and}\qquad \ell_{11}(Q) \sim R^n.
    </me>
    Hence <m>n = k m</m> and <m>\ell_{11}(Q) = \lambda \ell_{11}(P^k)</m>, for some <m>\lambda \in K^{\times}</m>. It follows that 
    <m>Q_1 := Q - \lambda P^k</m> satisfies the inequality <m>v_{11}(Q_1) &lt; v_{11}(Q)</m>. Moreover, it is clear that 
    <m>[P, Q_1] = [P,Q] \sim 1</m> and <m>K[P, Q_1] = K[P,Q]</m>. Hence, by version 3 of the Jacobian conjecture, 
    <m>(P, Q_1)</m> is also a counterexample. Now, we can construct successively <m>Q_2, Q_3, \dots</m>, such that 
    <m>(P, Q_j)</m> are counterexamples and <m>v_{11}(Q_j) &lt; v_{11}(Q_{j-1})</m>, until <m>v_{11}(P)</m> does not divide 
    <m>v_{11}(Q_j)</m>. Then
    <me>
      \gcd(v_{11}(P), v_{11}(Q_j)) &lt; v_{11}(P) = \gcd(v_{11}(P), v_{11}(Q)),
    </me>
    and so <m>\tilde{Q} := Q_j</m> and <m>\tilde{P} = P</m> satisfy the required conditions. When 
    <m>v_{11}(Q) \mid v_{11}(P)</m> a similar argument gives the proof.
  </p>
</proof>

<definition xml:id="counterexample_sin_divisibilidad">
  <statement>
    <p>
      A <term>counterexample <m>(P,Q)</m>, without divisibility relations of degrees</term> or simply a 
      <term>counterexample <m>(P,Q)</m>, without divisibility</term> 
      <idx>counterexample <m>(P,Q)</m>, without divisibility</idx>, is a counterexample to the Jacobian conjecture, 
      such that <m>v_{11}(P) \nmid v_{11}(Q)</m> and <m>v_{11}(Q) \nmid v_{11}(P)</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>

<lemma xml:id="lemma_PQ_similares_en_I">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Assume that the Jacobian conjecture is false, let <m>(P,Q)</m> be a counterexample to it and let 
      <m>(\rho,\sigma) \in [(1,-1),(0,1)] \subseteq \mathfrak{V}</m>. Assume that there exist coprime 
      <m>m,n \in \mathbb{N}</m>, such that
      <men xml:id="condicion1">
        \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(Q) = \frac{n}{m} \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(P).
      </men>
      Then
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><m>v_{\rho\sigma}(Q) = \frac{n}{m} v_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m>,</li>
      <li><m>v_{\rho\sigma}(P), v_{\rho\sigma}(Q) &gt; 0</m>,</li>
      <li><m>[\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P), \ell_{\rho\sigma}(Q)] = 0</m>,</li>
      <li><m>(\rho,\sigma) \in \operatorname{Dir}(P)</m> if and only if <m>(\rho,\sigma) \in \operatorname{Dir}(Q)</m>,</li>
      <li><m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(Q) = \frac{n}{m} \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m>.</li>
    </ol>
  </statement>
</lemma>

<proof>
  <p>
    To begin with note that <m>[(1,-1),(0,1)]</m> is contained in a half circle, and so, by 
    <xref ref="direcciones_consecutivas_prime"/>
    <me>
      (1,-1) &lt; (\rho,\sigma) \Leftrightarrow (1,-1) \times (\rho,\sigma) &gt; 0 \Leftrightarrow \rho + \sigma &gt; 0,
    </me>
    and
    <me>
      (\rho,\sigma) \le (0,1) \Leftrightarrow (\rho,\sigma) \times (0,1) \ge 0 \Leftrightarrow \rho \ge 0.
    </me>
    We next prove items 1--5 separately.
  </p>
  <p>
    <bold>1.</bold> By equality <xref ref="condicion1"/>, we have
    <me>
      v_{\rho\sigma}(Q) = v_{\rho\sigma}(\operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(Q))
      = v_{\rho\sigma}\left(\frac{n}{m} \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(P)\right)
      = \frac{n}{m} v_{\rho\sigma}(P).
    </me>
  </p>
  <p>
    <bold>2.</bold> Assume by contradiction that this is false. Then, by item 1, we have 
    <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(P) \le 0</m> and <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(Q) \le 0</m>. Hence, by 
    <xref ref="imposible_que_P_y_Q_esten_encima_de_la_diagonal"/>, necessarily <m>(\rho,\sigma) \ne (1,-1)</m>. 
    Consequently <m>\rho + \sigma &gt; 0</m>, and so, by <xref ref="dfpvc"/>
    <me>
      v_{\rho\sigma}([P,Q]) \le v_{\rho\sigma}(P) + v_{\rho\sigma}(Q) - (\rho + \sigma) \le -(\rho + \sigma) &lt; 0,
    </me>
    which is impossible, since <m>v_{\rho\sigma}([P,Q]) = 0</m>. This concludes the proof of item 2.
  </p>
  <p>
    <bold>3.</bold> By <xref ref="pr_v_de_un_conmutador"/>, we must prove that
    <me>
      v_{\rho\sigma}(P) + v_{\rho\sigma}(Q) - (\rho + \sigma) &gt; 0.
    </me>
    We first assume that <m>\sigma \ge 0</m>, and we consider the three cases:
    <ol>
      <li>a) <m>\rho = \sigma = 1</m>,</li>
      <li>b) <m>\rho &gt; \sigma</m>,</li>
      <li>c) <m>\rho &lt; \sigma</m>.</li>
    </ol>
    In the case a), from <xref ref="casos_pequenhos"/> we obtain directly
    <me>
      v_{11}(P) + v_{11}(Q) - (1+1) = \deg(P) + \deg(Q) - 2 &gt; 0,
    </me>
    as desired. In the case b), we note that, again by <xref ref="casos_pequenhos"/>, we know that <m>P \notin K[y]</m>, 
    and so there is a <m>(i,j) \in \operatorname{Supp}(P)</m> with <m>i \ge 1</m>. But then, since <m>j\sigma \ge 0</m>, we have
    <me>
      v_{\rho\sigma}(P) \ge v_{\rho\sigma}(i,j) = i\rho + j\sigma \ge i\rho \ge \rho,
    </me>
    and by the same argument <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(Q) \ge \rho</m>. Hence,
    <me>
      v_{\rho\sigma}(P) + v_{\rho\sigma}(Q) - (\rho + \sigma) \ge \rho - \sigma &gt; 0,
    </me>
    as desired. The case c) is similar (use that <m>\rho \ge 0</m>).
  </p>
  <p>
    Assume now that <m>\sigma &lt; 0</m>. We first note that there exists <m>(i,j) \in \operatorname{Supp}(P) \cup \operatorname{Supp}(Q)</m> 
    with <m>i &gt; j</m>. In fact, otherwise <m>v_{1,-1}(P) \le 0</m> and <m>v_{1,-1}(Q) \le 0</m>, which is impossible by 
    <xref ref="imposible_que_P_y_Q_esten_encima_de_la_diagonal"/>. If <m>(i,j) \in \operatorname{Supp}(P)</m>, then by 
    <xref ref="ve_rho_sigma_es_mayor_que_el_de_F"/>, we have
    <me>
      v_{\rho\sigma}(P) \ge v_{\rho\sigma}(i,j) &gt; \rho + \sigma.
    </me>
    Combining this with item 2, we obtain that
    <me>
      v_{\rho\sigma}(P) + v_{\rho\sigma}(Q) - (\rho + \sigma) &gt; v_{\rho\sigma}(Q) &gt; 0,
    </me>
    as desired. In the case <m>(i,j) \in \operatorname{Supp}(Q)</m>, the same argument works.
  </p>
  <p>
    <bold>4. and 5.</bold> By items 2 and 3, and by <xref ref="P_y_Q_alineados"/>(2b), there exist coprime 
    <m>n_1, m_1 \in \mathbb{N}</m>, and a <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous polynomial <m>R</m>, such that
    <me>
      \ell_{\rho\sigma}(P) \sim R^{m_1} \quad\text{and}\quad \ell_{\rho\sigma}(Q) \sim R^{n_1}.
    </me>
    Clearly <m>\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m> is a monomial if and only if <m>R</m> is a monomial, and this happens if and only if 
    <m>\ell_{\rho\sigma}(Q)</m> is a monomial, which proves item 4. Now, by <xref ref="st_y_en"/> we have
    <me>
      \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(P) = m_1 \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(R)
      \quad\text{and}\quad
      \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(Q) = n_1 \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(R).
    </me>
    Consequently, <m>\operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(Q) = \frac{n_1}{m_1} \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m>, and so, 
    <m>m_1 = m</m> and <m>n_1 = n</m>, by <xref ref="condicion1"/>. Finally, the same remark yields
    <me>
      \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P) = m \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(R)
      \quad\text{and}\quad
      \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(Q) = n \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(R),
    </me>
    which finishes the proof of item 5.
  </p>
</proof>

<proposition xml:id="star">
  <statement>
    <p>
      If <m>(P,Q)</m> is a counterexample to the plane Jacobian conjecture, then there exist coprime 
      <m>m,n \in \mathbb{N}</m> such that, for all <m>(\rho,\sigma) \in [(1,-1),(0,1)]</m>, we have
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><m>v_{\rho\sigma}(Q) = \frac{n}{m} v_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m>,</li>
      <li><m>v_{\rho\sigma}(P) &gt; 0</m> and <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(Q) &gt; 0</m>,</li>
      <li><m>[\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P), \ell_{\rho\sigma}(Q)] = 0</m>,</li>
      <li>
        <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(Q) = \frac{n}{m} \operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m> and 
        <m>\operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(Q) = \frac{n}{m} \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m>,
      </li>
      <li>
        <m>\operatorname{Dir}(P) \cap [(1,-1),(0,1)] = \operatorname{Dir}(Q) \cap [(1,-1),(0,1)]</m>.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </statement>
</proposition>

<proof>
  <p>
    Set <m>\mathcal{D} := \bigl(\operatorname{Dir}(P) \cup \operatorname{Dir}(Q)\bigr) \cap [(1,-1),(0,1)[</m> and write
    <me>
      \mathcal{D} \cup \{(0,1)\} = \{(\rho_0,\sigma_0), \dots, (\rho_k,\sigma_k)\}
      \quad\text{with } (\rho_j,\sigma_j) &lt; (\rho_{j+1},\sigma_{j+1}), \text{ for all } j = 0,\dots,k-1.
    </me>
    We first consider the case <m>(\rho,\sigma) = (\rho_k,\sigma_k) = (0,1)</m>. By <xref ref="casos_pequenhos"/>, we have 
    <m>P,Q \in K[x,y]\setminus K[x]</m>, which implies <m>v_{01}(P) \ge 1</m> and <m>v_{01}(Q) \ge 1</m>. Hence,
    <me>
      v_{01}(P) + v_{01}(Q) - (0+1) \ge 1 &gt; 0 = v_{01}([P,Q]),
    </me>
    and so, by <xref ref="pr_v_de_un_conmutador"/>, we know that <m>[\ell_{01}(P), \ell_{01}(Q)] = 0</m>. Thus items 2 and 3 
    are satisfied, and so, by <xref ref="P_y_Q_alineados"/>(2b), there exist coprime <m>n,m \in \mathbb{N}</m>, and a 
    <m>(0,1)</m>-homogeneous polynomial <m>R</m> such that
    <me>
      \ell_{01}(P) \sim R^m \quad\text{and}\quad \ell_{01}(Q) \sim R^n.
    </me>
    Then, by <xref ref="st_y_en"/>, we have
    <me>
      \operatorname{stn}_{01}(P) = m \operatorname{stn}_{01}(R) \qquad\text{and}\qquad 
      \operatorname{stn}_{01}(Q) = n \operatorname{stn}_{01}(R),
    </me>
    which yields <m>\operatorname{stn}_{01}(Q) = \frac{n}{m} \operatorname{stn}_{01}(P)</m>. Similarly 
    <m>\operatorname{en}_{01}(Q) = \frac{n}{m} \operatorname{en}_{01}(P)</m>, which finishes the proof of item 4, for 
    <m>(\rho,\sigma) = (0,1)</m>. Moreover, we also have
    <me>
      v_{01}(P) = v_{01}(\operatorname{stn}_{01}(P)) = v_{01}\left(\frac{m}{n} \operatorname{stn}_{01}(Q)\right)
      = \frac{m}{n} v_{01}(Q),
    </me>
    which proves item 1 in this case. Assume inductively that <m>0 \le j &lt; k</m> and that items 1--4 hold for the direction 
    <m>(\rho_{j+1},\sigma_{j+1})</m>. Then
    <me>
      \operatorname{en}_{\rho_j,\sigma_j}(Q) = \operatorname{stn}_{\rho_{j+1},\sigma_{j+1}}(Q)
      = \frac{n}{m} \operatorname{stn}_{\rho_{j+1},\sigma_{j+1}}(P)
      = \frac{n}{m} \operatorname{en}_{\rho_j,\sigma_j}(P),
    </me>
    and <xref ref="lemma_PQ_similares_en_I"/> yields items 1--4, for <m>(\rho_j,\sigma_j)</m>. Hence items 1--4 hold for all 
    <m>(\rho_i,\sigma_i)</m>. Moreover, item 4 of <xref ref="lemma_PQ_similares_en_I"/> guarantees that
    <me>
      \operatorname{Dir}(P) \cap [(1,-1),(0,1)] = \operatorname{Dir}(Q) \cap [(1,-1),(0,1)],
    </me>
    proving item 5. Finally take <m>(\rho,\sigma) \in [(1,-1),(0,1)[</m>, and notice that for some <m>j</m>, we have
    <me>
      \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(Q) = \operatorname{stn}_{\rho_j,\sigma_j}(Q)
      = \frac{n}{m} \operatorname{stn}_{\rho_j,\sigma_j}(P)
      = \frac{n}{m} \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(P),
    </me>
    and, again by <xref ref="lemma_PQ_similares_en_I"/>, we obtain items 1--4, for <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>, concluding the proof.
  </p>
</proof>


  <figure xml:id="Ilustracion_de_star">
  <caption>Illustration of <xref ref="star"/></caption>
  <image width="60%" source="img/img20.png"/>
</figure>

<proposition xml:id="direcciones_1k">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>(P,Q)</m> be a Jacobian pair in <m>K[x,y]</m>, let <m>(\rho,\sigma) \in ](1,0),(0,1)[ \cap \operatorname{Dir}(P)</m> 
      and let <m>F</m> be as in <xref ref="central"/>. Then the following cases arise:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>If <m>(\rho,\sigma) = (1,1)</m>, then <m>\operatorname{Supp}(F) \subseteq \{(0,2),(1,1),(2,0)\}</m>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          If <m>(1,1) &lt; (\rho,\sigma) &lt; (0,1)</m>, then <m>(\rho,\sigma) = (1,k)</m> for some <m>k &gt; 1</m>, 
          <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(F) = (k+1,0)</m> and <m>\operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(F) = (1,1)</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          If <m>(1,0) &lt; (\rho,\sigma) &lt; (1,1)</m>, then <m>(\rho,\sigma) = (k,1)</m> for some <m>k &gt; 1</m>, 
          <m>\operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(F) = (0,k+1)</m> and <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(F) = (1,1)</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Moreover, if <m>(\rho,\sigma) \ne (1,1)</m> and if we write <m>F = x^u y^v f(z)</m>, where 
      <m>z = x^{-\sigma} y^{\rho}</m> and <m>(u,v) = \operatorname{stn}_{\rho,\sigma}(F)</m>, then <m>f(z)</m> has exactly 
      one linear factor (which is different from <m>z</m>, since <m>f(0) \ne 0</m>, by <xref ref="polinomio_asociado_f"/>).
    </p>
  </statement>
</proposition>

<proof>
  <p>
    Note that <m>(1,0) &lt; (\rho,\sigma) &lt; (0,1)</m> if and only if <m>\rho,\sigma &gt; 0</m>, and 
    <m>(\rho,\sigma) \in \operatorname{Dir}(P)</m> if and only if <m>\ell_{\rho\sigma}(P)</m> is not a monomial. By 
    <xref ref="central_2"/>(3) the <m>(\rho,\sigma)</m>-homogeneous polynomial <m>F</m> is not a monomial, and will find 
    the line <m>L</m> containing <m>\operatorname{Supp}(F)</m>. Since <m>v_{\rho\sigma}(F) = \rho + \sigma</m>, we know 
    that <m>L</m> is the straight line going through <m>(1,1)</m> with slope <m>a = -\frac{\rho}{\sigma}</m> 
    (see <xref ref="posibles_L"/>). If <m>(\rho,\sigma) = (1,1)</m>, then clearly
    <me>
      \operatorname{Supp}(F) \subseteq \{(0,2),(1,1),(2,0)\},
    </me>
    proving item 1. If <m>(1,1) &lt; (\rho,\sigma) &lt; (0,1)</m>, then the slope <m>a</m> is in <m>]-1,0[</m> and 
    <m>L</m> intersects <m>\mathbb{N}_0 \times \mathbb{N}_0</m> in more than one point only if 
    <m>(\rho,\sigma) = (1,k)</m>, for some <m>k \in \mathbb{N}</m>. In this case
    <me>
      \operatorname{Supp}(F) = \{(1,1), (k+1,0)\},
    </me>
    and then <m>f(z)</m> has degree <m>1</m>, <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(F) = (k+1,0)</m> and 
    <m>\operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(F) = (1,1)</m>. Finally, if <m>(1,0) &lt; (\rho,\sigma) &lt; (1,1)</m>, then the slope 
    <m>a</m> is in <m>]-\infty,-1[</m> and <m>L</m> intersects <m>\mathbb{N}_0 \times \mathbb{N}_0</m> in more than one 
    point only if <m>(\rho,\sigma) = (k,1)</m>, for some <m>k \in \mathbb{N}</m>. In this case
    <me>
      \operatorname{Supp}(F) = \{(1,1), (0,k+1)\},
    </me>
    and then <m>f(z)</m> has degree <m>1</m>, <m>\operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(F) = (0,k+1)</m> and 
    <m>\operatorname{stn}_{\rho\sigma}(F) = (1,1)</m>.
  </p>
</proof>

<figure xml:id="posibles_L">
  <caption>Lines <m>L</m> through <m>(1,1)</m> that intersect <m>\mathbb{N}_0 \times \mathbb{N}_0</m> in more than one point</caption>
  <image width="60%" source="img/img21.png"/>
</figure>

<remark xml:id="casos_soporte">
  <p>
    Assume that <m>(P,Q)</m> is a Jacobian pair and let <m>F</m> be the <m>(1,1)</m>-homogeneous polynomial given in 
    <xref ref="central"/>. Write
    <me>
      \ell_{11}(P) = x^i y^j p(z) \quad\text{and}\quad F = x^u y^v f(z),
    </me>
    where <m>z := x^{-1} y</m> and <m>p,f \in K[z]</m> are polynomials such that <m>p(0) \ne 0</m> and <m>f(0) \ne 0</m>. 
    By <xref ref="direcciones_1k"/>(1), <m>\operatorname{Supp}(F) \subseteq \{(0,2),(1,1),(2,0)\}</m>, and so by 
    <xref ref="central_2"/>, the polynomial <m>p(z)</m> has at most two distinct linear factors.
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <p>
        If <m>p(z)</m> has zero linear factors, i.e., if <m>p(z)</m> is a constant, then <m>\ell_{11}(P)</m> is a monomial 
        with support equal to <m>\{(i,j)\}</m>, and we have the following three possibilities, which are illustrated in 
        <xref ref="p_constante"/>:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>a) <m>i \ge 1</m> and <m>j \ge 1</m>,</li>
        <li>b) <m>i = 0</m> and <m>j &gt; 0</m>,</li>
        <li>c) <m>i &gt; 0</m> and <m>j = 0</m>.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        If <m>p(z)</m> is a power of a linear factor, then <m>f</m> has at least one linear factor, and so 
        <m>(0,2) \in \operatorname{Supp}(F)</m> or <m>(2,0) \in \operatorname{Supp}(F)</m>. Since, by 
        <xref ref="sobre_central"/>, we know that <m>\operatorname{en}_{11}(P) \sim \operatorname{en}_{11}(F)</m> if 
        <m>\operatorname{en}_{11}(F) \ne (1,1)</m> and <m>\operatorname{stn}_{11}(P) \sim \operatorname{stn}_{11}(F)</m> 
        if <m>\operatorname{stn}_{11}(F) \ne (1,1)</m>, we have three possibilities, which are illustrated in 
        <xref ref="p_de_con_un_factor"/>:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>a) <m>\operatorname{stn}_{11}(P) \sim (2,0)</m> and <m>\operatorname{en}_{11}(P) \sim (0,2)</m>,</li>
        <li>b) <m>\operatorname{stn}_{11}(P) \sim (2,0)</m> and <m>\operatorname{en}_{11}(P) \nsim (0,2)</m>,</li>
        <li>c) <m>\operatorname{stn}_{11}(P) \nsim (2,0)</m> and <m>\operatorname{en}_{11}(P) \sim (0,2)</m>.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        If <m>p(z)</m> has two different linear factors, then <m>f</m> has also two different linear factors, and hence 
        <m>(0,2),(2,0) \in \operatorname{Supp}(F)</m>. Again by <xref ref="sobre_central"/>, necessarily 
        <m>\operatorname{stn}_{11}(P) \sim (2,0)</m> and <m>\operatorname{en}_{11}(P) \sim (0,2)</m>. This is also 
        illustrated in <xref ref="p_de_con_un_factor"/>.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</remark>

<figure xml:id="p_constante">
  <caption>Case 1</caption>
  <image width="80%" source="img/img22.png"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="p_de_con_un_factor">
  <caption>Case 2 and Case 3</caption>
  <image width="80%" source="img/img23.png"/>
</figure>

<proposition xml:id="reduccion_con_un_factor">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Assume the Jacobian conjecture is false and let <m>(P,Q)</m> be a counterexample to it. Assume that 
      <m>v_{11}(P)</m> and <m>v_{11}(Q)</m> do not divide each other and that we are in one of the cases 1b), 1c) or 2a) 
      of <xref ref="casos_soporte"/>. Then there exists an automorphism <m>\varphi</m> of <m>K[x,y]</m> such that
      <men xml:id="faltaba">
        \frac{v_{11}(\varphi(P))}{v_{11}(\varphi(Q))} = \frac{v_{11}(P)}{v_{11}(Q)}
        \quad\text{and}\quad
        v_{11}(\varphi(P)) &lt; v_{11}(P).
      </men>
      Consequently, <m>\gcd(\deg(\varphi(P)), \deg(\varphi(Q))) &lt; \gcd(\deg(P), \deg(Q))</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</proposition>

<proof>
  <p>
    If we are in the case 2a), then <m>\operatorname{stn}_{11}(P) \sim (2,0)</m> and so, by 
    <xref ref="calculo_de_st_y_en"/> or (1), we are in the case c) of <xref ref="casos_shaping"/> and we can erase the 
    edge <m>\ell_{11}(P)</m>. By this and <xref ref="ejemplo_de_auto_homo"/>, we arrive at case 1b) without changing 
    <m>v_{11}(P)</m> nor <m>v_{11}(Q)</m>. Now, if we are in the case 1b), then we can apply the automorphism which 
    exchanges <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, and we arrive at case 1c) without changing <m>v_{11}(P)</m> nor <m>v_{11}(Q)</m>. 
    Thus, we can assume that we are in the case 1c). Then, by <xref ref="casos_pequenhos"/>,
    <me>
      (\rho,\sigma) := \operatorname{Succ}_P(1,1) \in ](1,1),(0,1)[.
    </me>
    So, by <xref ref="direcciones_1k"/>(2), we have <m>(\rho,\sigma) = (1,k)</m>, for some <m>k \ge 2</m>. Moreover, by 
    <xref ref="star"/>(2), we know that <m>v_{1k}(P) &gt; 0</m>. Let <m>F</m> be as in <xref ref="central"/> and write
    <me>
      \ell_{1k}(P) = x^r y^s p(z) \quad\text{and}\quad F = x^u y^v f(z),
    </me>
    where <m>z := x^{-k} y</m> and <m>p,f \in K[z]</m> are polynomials such that <m>p(0) \ne 0</m> and <m>f(0) \ne 0</m>. 
    Again by <xref ref="direcciones_1k"/>, we also know that <m>f(z) \sim (z - \lambda)</m>, for some 
    <m>\lambda \in K^{\times}</m>, and so, using <xref ref="central_2"/>, we obtain that <m>p(z) \sim (z - \lambda)^t</m>, 
    for some <m>t &gt; 0</m>. Moreover, since <m>(r,s) = \operatorname{stn}_{1k}(P) = \operatorname{en}_{11}(P)</m> and 
    we are in the case 1c), we know that <m>s = 0</m>. Hence
    <men xml:id="para_despues1">
      v_{11}(P) = v_{11}(\operatorname{en}_{11}(P)) = v_{11}(r,0) = r
      \quad\text{and}\quad
      \ell_{1k}(P) = \mu x^r (z - \lambda)^t = \mu x^{r - tk} (y - \lambda x^k)^t.
    </men>
    We define <m>\varphi</m> by setting <m>\varphi(x) := x</m> and <m>\varphi(y) := y + \lambda x^k</m>, and set 
    <m>P_1 := \varphi(P)</m> and <m>Q_1 := \varphi(Q)</m>. By <xref ref="pr_ell_por_automorfismos"/>
    <men xml:id="para_despues2">
      \ell_{1k}(P_1) = \varphi\bigl(\ell_{1k}(P)\bigr) = \mu x^{r - tk} y^t
      \qquad\text{and}\qquad
      v_{1k}(P_1) = v_{1k}(P).
    </men>
    Hence, for each <m>(i,j) \in \operatorname{Supp}(P_1)</m> we have
    <me>
      v_{1k}(i,j) = i + jk \le v_{1k}(P_1) = v_{1k}(P) = r,
    </me>
    which, combined with the fact that <m>k \ge 1</m>, gives
    <me>
      v_{11}(i,j) = i + j \le i + jk \le r = v_{11}(P).
    </me>
    Since <m>k &gt; 1</m>, the equality is only possible if <m>j = 0</m> and <m>i = r</m>. But it is impossible that 
    <m>(r,0) \in \operatorname{Supp}(P_1)</m>. In fact, since <m>v_{1k}(r,0) = r = v_{1k}(P_1)</m>, if 
    <m>(r,0) \in \operatorname{Supp}(P_1)</m>, then <m>(r,0) \in \operatorname{Supp}(\ell_{1k}(P_1))</m>, which is false 
    because <m>t &gt; 0</m>. Consequently, <m>v_{11}(P_1) &lt; v_{11}(P)</m>, which is the second condition in 
    <xref ref="faltaba"/>. The first condition follows from the series of equalities
    <me>
      \frac{v_{11}(P_1)}{v_{11}(Q_1)} = \frac{v_{1k}(P_1)}{v_{1k}(Q_1)}
      = \frac{v_{1k}(P)}{v_{1k}(Q)} = \frac{v_{11}(P)}{v_{11}(Q)},
    </me>
    where the first and the third one are true by <xref ref="star"/>(1); and the second one, by 
    <xref ref="pr_ell_por_automorfismos"/>.
  </p>
</proof>

<definition xml:id="subrectangular">
  <statement>
    <p>
      We will say that <m>P \in K[x,y]</m> is <term>subrectangular</term> <idx>subrectangular polynomial</idx> if there 
      exists <m>a,b \in \mathbb{N}</m> such that
      <me>
        (a,b) \in \operatorname{Supp}(P) \subseteq \{(i,j) : 0 \le i \le a \text{ and } 0 \le j \le b\}.
      </me>
      In this case we also will say that <m>\operatorname{Supp}(P)</m> is <term>subrectangular</term> 
      <idx>subrectangular support</idx>. Finally, we will say that a pair <m>(P,Q)</m> is subrectangular if 
      <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> are. <idx>subrectangular pair of polynomials</idx>
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>

<p>
  Note that <m>P \in K[x,y]</m> is subrectangular if and only if <m>P \notin K[x] \cup K[y]</m>, and either <m>P</m> is a 
  monomial or <m>\operatorname{Succ}_P(1,0) \ge (0,1)</m>.
</p>

<definition xml:id="standard_counterexample">
  <statement>
    <p>
      A counterexample <m>(P,Q)</m> to the Jacobian conjecture is called a <term>standard counterexample</term> 
      <idx>standard counterexample</idx> if <m>\operatorname{Succ}_P(1,0) &gt; (0,1)</m> and there exists 
      <m>m,n \in \mathbb{N}</m> such that <m>(P,Q)</m> is a standard <m>(m,n)</m>-pair.
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>

<remark>
  <p>
    The pair <m>(m,n)</m> in the previous definition is unique and coincides with the pair <m>(m,n)</m> in 
    <xref ref="star"/>.
  </p>
</remark>

<proposition xml:id="contraejemplo_da_standard_mn_par">
  <statement>
    <p>
      For each counterexample <m>(P,Q)</m> such that <m>v_{11}(P)</m> and <m>v_{11}(Q)</m> do not divide each other, 
      there exists an automorphism <m>\varphi</m> of <m>K[x,y]</m> such that <m>(\varphi(P),\varphi(Q))</m> is a 
      subrectangular counterexample to the Jacobian Conjecture, which is a standard <m>(m,n)</m>-pair, for some 
      <m>m,n \in \mathbb{N}</m>. Moreover
      <men xml:id="preserva_cociente_y_no_sube_grado">
        \frac{v_{11}(\varphi(P))}{v_{11}(\varphi(Q))} = \frac{v_{11}(P)}{v_{11}(Q)}
        \quad\text{and}\quad
        v_{11}(\varphi(P)) \le v_{11}(P).
      </men>
    </p>
  </statement>
</proposition>

<proof>
  <p>
    By <xref ref="reduccion_con_un_factor"/>, applying an automorphism of <m>K[x,y]</m> if necessary, we can assume that 
    we are not in any of the cases 1b), 1c) or 2a) of <xref ref="casos_soporte"/>. Now, we will modify <m>P</m> and 
    <m>Q</m> without changing their degrees, either by using <xref ref="pr_ell_por_automorfismos"/> or by applying 
    <m>\varphi_{y,x}</m>. Hence condition <xref ref="preserva_cociente_y_no_sube_grado"/> will be satisfied. If we are 
    in case 3), then
    <me>
      \ell_{11}(P) = x^n (z - \lambda_1)^{n_1} (z - \lambda_2)^{n_2}
      \quad \text{with } z := x^{-1} y,\; \lambda_1,\lambda_2 \in K^{\times},\; \lambda_1 \ne \lambda_2,\; n_1,n_2 &gt; 0
      \text{ and } n_1 + n_2 = n,
    </me>
    where for the last equality we have used (2). Applying the automorphism <m>\varphi</m> of <m>K[x,y]</m>, defined by 
    <m>\varphi(x) := x</m> and <m>\varphi(y) := y + \lambda_1 x</m>, we arrive at case 2c). If we are in case 2c), then 
    we apply the automorphism which exchanges <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, and we arrive at case 2b). In case 2b) we can erase 
    the edge <m>\ell_{11}(P)</m> (see <xref ref="casos_shaping"/> c)) and arrive at case 1a). Thus, we can and will assume 
    that we are in case 1a), and so we have
    <me>
      \ell_{11}(P) \sim x^{ma} y^{mb} \quad\text{and}\quad \ell_{11}(Q) \sim x^{na} y^{nb}
      \quad \text{with } a,b,n,m \in \mathbb{N} \text{ and } m,n \text{ coprime},
    </me>
    where we have used <xref ref="star"/>(5). If necessary, we apply <m>\varphi_{y,x}</m> to ensure that <m>a \le b</m>. 
    Since <xref ref="central"/>(4) guarantees that <m>a \ne b</m>, we conclude that <m>a &lt; b</m>. We claim that
    <me>
      \operatorname{Pred}_{11}(P) \le (1,0) \quad\text{and}\quad \operatorname{Succ}_{11}(P) \ge (0,1).
    </me>
    In fact, to justify the first inequality, suppose for contradiction that 
    <m>(\rho,\sigma) = \operatorname{Pred}_{11}(P) \in ](1,0),(1,1)[</m> and let <m>F</m> be as in 
    <xref ref="central"/>. By <xref ref="direcciones_1k"/>(2) we know that 
    <m>\operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(F) = (0,k+1)</m> with <m>k &gt; 1</m>. Consequently 
    <m>\operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(F) \ne (1,1)</m> and thus, by <xref ref="sobre_central"/>, we obtain
    <me>
      (0,k+1) = \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(F) \sim \operatorname{en}_{\rho\sigma}(P) = \operatorname{stn}_{11}(P) = (ma,mb).
    </me>
    This leads to a contradiction, and thus we conclude that <m>\operatorname{Pred}_{11}(P) \le (1,0)</m>, as claimed. 
    Similarly one shows that <m>\operatorname{Succ}_{11}(P) \ge (0,1)</m>. Consequently
    <me>
      (ma,mb) = \operatorname{stn}_{01}(P) = \operatorname{en}_{10}(P),
    </me>
    which implies that both <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> are subrectangular. Hence,
    <me>
      [P,Q] \in K^{\times},\quad \frac{v_{10}(P)}{v_{10}(Q)} = \frac{v_{11}(P)}{v_{11}(Q)} = \frac{m}{n}
      \quad\text{and}\quad v_{1,-1}(\operatorname{en}_{10}(P)) &lt; 0,
    </me>
    where the last inequality follows from the fact that <m>a &lt; b</m>. Moreover, since <m>v_{11}(P)</m> and 
    <m>v_{11}(Q)</m> do not divide each other, it follows that <m>m,n &gt; 1</m>, and therefore <m>(P,Q)</m> is an 
    <m>(m,n)</m>-pair. Let <m>F</m> be as in <xref ref="central"/> corresponding to the direction <m>(1,0)</m>. Since 
    <m>v_{10}(F) = 1</m>, we may write <m>F \sim x f(y)</m>, where <m>f</m> is a monic polynomial. Moreover, following 
    <xref ref="casos_shaping"/>, we write
    <me>
      \ell_{10}(P) \sim x^{ma} y^{m_0} \prod_{i=1}^r (y - \lambda_i)^{m_i},
    </me>
    where <m>\lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_r \in K^{\times}</m> are pairwise distinct, <m>m_0 \in \mathbb{N}_0</m>, 
    <m>m_1,\dots,m_r \in \mathbb{N}</m> and <m>\sum_{i=0}^r m_i = mb</m>. If <m>m_0 = mb</m>, then <m>\ell_{10}(P)</m> 
    is a monomial. In this case we have <m>v_{1,-1}(\operatorname{stn}_{10}(P)) = v_{1,-1}(\operatorname{en}_{10}(P)) &lt; 0</m>, 
    and therefore <m>(P,Q)</m> is a standard <m>(m,n)</m>-pair. If <m>r = 1</m> and <m>m_1 = mb</m>, then, as noted in 
    <xref ref="casos_shaping"/>(c), the edge <m>\ell_{10}(P)</m> can be erased. In this case, <m>\ell_{10}(P)</m> becomes 
    a monomial, which implies that <m>v_{1,-1}(\operatorname{stn}_{10}(P)) &lt; 0</m>, and therefore <m>(P,Q)</m> becomes 
    a standard <m>(m,n)</m>-pair. Consequently, we can assume that <m>r &gt; 1</m>, or <m>r = 1</m> and <m>m_0 &gt; 0</m>. 
    We claim that
    <men xml:id="pepe5">
      r \le \begin{cases} c &amp; \text{if } m_0 = 0,\\ c-1 &amp; \text{if } m_0 &gt; 0, \end{cases}
    </men>
    where <m>c := \deg f</m>. By <xref ref="central_2"/>(2), to prove this bound it suffices to show that if 
    <m>m_0 &gt; 0</m>, then <m>f(0) = 0</m>. Assume for contradiction, that <m>m_0 &gt; 0</m> and <m>f(0) \ne 0</m>. 
    Then <m>\operatorname{stn}_{10}(F) = (1,0) \ne (1,1)</m>, and by <xref ref="sobre_central"/>, we would have
    <me>
      (1,0) = \operatorname{stn}_{10}(F) \sim \operatorname{stn}_{10}(P) = (ma,m_0),
    </me>
    which leads to a contradiction. Now observe that the bound in <xref ref="pepe5"/> implies <m>c &gt; 1</m>. Consequently, 
    <m>\operatorname{en}_{10}(F) = (1,c) \ne (1,1)</m>. Therefore, by <xref ref="sobre_central"/>, we have
    <me>
      (ma,mb) = \operatorname{en}_{10}(P) \sim \operatorname{en}_{10}(F) = (1,c),
    </me>
    and thus <m>b = ac</m>. But then for some index <m>j</m> we have <m>m_j \ge ma</m>; otherwise, by 
    <xref ref="pepe5"/> we would derive the contradiction
    <me>
      mb = \sum_{i=0}^r m_i &lt; c ma = mb.
    </me>
    Let <m>\varphi</m> be the automorphism of <m>K[x,y]</m> defined by <m>\varphi(x) := x</m> and 
    <m>\varphi(y) := y - \lambda_j</m>. By <xref ref="pr_ell_por_automorfismos"/>, we have
    <me>
      \ell_{10}(\varphi(P)) = \varphi(\ell_{10}(P)) \sim x^{ma} y^{m_j} \prod_{i \ne j} (y - \lambda_i + \lambda_j)^{m_i},
    </me>
    and therefore, by <xref ref="calculo_de_st_y_en"/>, it follows that 
    <m>\operatorname{stn}_{10}(\varphi(P)) = (ma, m_j)</m>. Now observe that <m>m_j = ma</m> is impossible; otherwise we 
    would have <m>\operatorname{stn}_{10}(\varphi(P)) = (ma,ma) \sim (1,1)</m>, contradicting 
    <xref ref="central"/>(4). Hence,
    <me>
      v_{1,-1}(\operatorname{stn}_{10}(\varphi(P))) = ma - m_j &lt; 0,
    </me>
    which shows that <m>(\varphi(P),\varphi(Q))</m> is a standard <m>(m,n)</m>-pair.
  </p>
</proof>
    <figure xml:id="factores_de_ell10">
  <caption>Factors of <m>\ell_{10}(P)</m></caption>
  <image width="90%" source="img/img24.png"/>
</figure>

<proposition xml:id="subrectangular_contraejemplo">
  <statement>
    <p>
      For each counterexample <m>(P,Q)</m> such that <m>v_{11}(P)</m> and <m>v_{11}(Q)</m> do not divide each other, 
      there exists an automorphism <m>\phi</m> of <m>K[x,y]</m> such that <m>(\phi(P),\phi(Q))</m> is a standard 
      counterexample to the Jacobian Conjecture,
      <men xml:id="preserva_cociente_y_no_sube_grado_prime">
        \frac{v_{11}(\phi(P))}{v_{11}(\phi(Q))} = \frac{v_{11}(P)}{v_{11}(Q)}
        \quad\text{and}\quad
        v_{11}(\phi(P)) \le v_{11}(P).
      </men>
    </p>
  </statement>
</proposition>

<proof>
  <p>
    By <xref ref="contraejemplo_da_standard_mn_par"/>, applying an automorphism of <m>K[x,y]</m> if necessary, we can 
    assume that <m>(P,Q)</m> is a subrectangular counterexample to the Jacobian Conjecture, which is a standard 
    <m>(m,n)</m>-pair, for some <m>m,n \in \mathbb{N}</m>. Write <m>(ma,mb) := \operatorname{stn}_{01}(P)</m>. If 
    <m>\operatorname{Succ}_P(1,0) &gt; (0,1)</m>, then there is nothing to prove. Suppose instead that 
    <m>\operatorname{Succ}_P(1,0) = (0,1)</m>. By <xref ref="star"/>(2), we know that <m>v_{01}(P) &gt; 0</m>. Let 
    <m>F</m> be the <m>(0,1)</m>-homogeneous polynomial provided by <xref ref="central"/>. Since 
    <m>v_{01}(F) = 1</m>, it follows that <m>\operatorname{stn}_{01}(F) = (u,1)</m>. We assert that <m>u = 1</m>. 
    Indeed, if <m>u \ne 1</m>, then by <xref ref="sobre_central"/>, it must be that 
    <m>(u,1) \sim (ma,mb)</m>, which leads to a contradiction, as <m>0 &lt; a &lt; b</m> and <m>u \in \mathbb{N}_0</m>. 
    Since <m>(0,1) \in \operatorname{Dir}(P)</m>, <xref ref="central_2"/>(3) ensures that <m>F</m> is not a monomial. 
    Hence, by <xref ref="calculo_de_st_y_en"/>, we obtain <m>\operatorname{en}_{01}(F) = (0,1)</m> and 
    <m>F \sim xy(x^{-1} - \lambda)</m> with <m>\lambda \in K^{\times}</m>. Consequently, by 
    <xref ref="central_2"/>(4), we conclude that
    <me>
      \ell_{01}(P) \sim x^{ma} y^{mb} (x^{-1} - \lambda)^{\ell}, \quad \text{for some } \ell \in \mathbb{N}.
    </me>
    According to <xref ref="calculo_de_st_y_en"/>, this implies that 
    <m>\operatorname{en}_{01}(P) = (ma - \ell, mb)</m>. On the other hand, applying <xref ref="sobre_central"/> once again, 
    we deduce that <m>(ma - \ell, mb) = \operatorname{en}_{01}(P) \sim (0,1)</m>, and therefore <m>\ell = ma</m>. 
    Let <m>\phi</m> be the automorphism of <m>K[x,y]</m>, defined by <m>\phi(x) := x + \lambda^{-1}</m> and 
    <m>\phi(y) := y</m>. By <xref ref="de_pr_ell_por_automorfismos"/>
    <me>
      \ell_{01}(\phi(P)) = \phi(\ell_{01}(P)) \sim x^{ma} y^{mb}, \quad
      \ell_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(\phi(P)) = \ell_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(P) \quad\text{and}\quad
      \ell_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(\phi(Q)) = \ell_{\rho_1,\sigma_1}(Q),
    </me>
    for each <m>(0,-1) &lt; (\rho_1,\sigma_1) &lt; (0,1)</m>. From this it follows that 
    <m>\operatorname{Succ}_{\phi(P)}(1,0) &gt; (0,1)</m>, that <m>(\phi(P),\phi(Q))</m> is a standard 
    <m>(m,n)</m>-pair and that <xref ref="preserva_cociente_y_no_sube_grado_prime"/> is satisfied.
  </p>
</proof>

